{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r1s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Shared radix-4 combinational divider with borrow-based digit selection\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline reaches only 5.442 MHz, with a 199.837 ns, 156-cell worst\n  path crossing operand forwarding and the combinational ALU. The four\n  signed/unsigned division and remainder expressions in alu.sv are the\n  strongest candidate for this deep arithmetic path. CoreMark fitness\n  scales directly with Fmax when instruction latency and cycle count stay\n  constant. There are no earlier bench hypotheses or recorded lessons.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with one shared, fully\n  combinational unsigned radix-4 restoring divider. Convert operands to\n  unsigned magnitudes for DIV and REM, and use the original bit patterns\n  for DIVU and REMU. Precompute widened divisor multiples B, 2B and 3B.\n  Unroll 16 digit stages, consuming the dividend from its most significant\n  pair of bits to its least significant pair. At each stage form the\n  widened partial dividend P = 4*remainder + next_pair, calculate P-B,\n  P-2B and P-3B in parallel, and select the largest nonnegative trial to\n  emit quotient digit 0, 1, 2 or 3 and the corresponding new remainder.\n  Derive trial validity from each widened subtraction's borrow bit rather\n  than instantiating a separate magnitude comparator followed by another\n  subtraction. Use sufficient unsigned width throughout: divisor\n  multiples and partial dividends require 34 bits, and trial differences\n  require 35 bits to retain underflow. Expose quotient and remainder from\n  the same network; apply quotient sign from a_sign XOR b_sign and\n  remainder sign from a_sign only for signed operations. Explicitly\n  override division by zero before sign correction: quotient is all ones\n  and remainder is the original dividend. Preserve INT_MIN/-1 results.\n  This targets fewer serial quotient-selection stages and shared arithmetic\n  to improve timing and routing area. Keep the combinational alu interface,\n  pipeline registers, forwarding, bus handshakes, instruction cycle counts,\n  nret=1 and all RVFI behavior unchanged. Preserve the existing\n  RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions verbatim and leave multiplication and\n  base integer operations unchanged. The main risk is that the three\n  parallel trials and their selection mux increase routing enough to\n  offset the shorter stage chain; vendor timing must verify the gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 25\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the shared combinational radix-4 div/rem network inside\n      the real-arithmetic branch, with explicitly widened parallel trial\n      subtractions, borrow-based quotient digit selection, signed magnitude\n      conversion and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow handling. Retain\n      the existing ALTOPS branch and all non-division ALU operations.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all current checks and add deterministic randomized and\n      boundary vectors for all four div/rem operations against an independent\n      Python integer reference using truncation toward zero. Exercise all\n      operand sign combinations, zero divisors, INT_MIN/-1, divisors with\n      bit 31 set, powers of two, values around radix-4 digit boundaries,\n      and consecutive switches between signed and unsigned operations.\n      Require the existing formal and stalled CoreMark cosim gates to pass,\n      then compare Gowin Fmax and fitness against the 4.6 percent threshold.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
