h to offset CoreMark's two extra MUL clocks without lengthening the seven-loop divider schedule.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r5s1 (improvement, +8.11%): Consuming five quotient bits per LOOP with parallel radix-8/radix-4 trials frees a PREP clock for preserved raw-operand registers, shortening the forwarding-to-divider path while preserving DIV/REM latency and CoreMark marker cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r6s0 (regression, +4.00%): Sharing SRL/SRA through a signed 33-bit `>>>` barrel shortens the ALU shift/result path without changing CoreMark cycles, but the 6.58% single-placement Fmax gain becomes only about 4% at the placement median, below the 4.6% acceptance bar.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r6s1 (improvement, +8.69%): Capturing a dedicated `rs1 + imm` address, replicated store data and alignment-qualified masks at the existing EX/MEM edge removes general ALU/result muxes and MEM-side width/alignment decoding from the memory request path without adding a pipeline stage.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.27%): Replacing the BHT's variable-index read-modify-write with bank/row-enabled local Boolean counter feedback preserves prediction behavior, so with the champion's critical path outside the predictor this topology cleanup offers no cycle savings and falls short of the 4.6% fitness threshold.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r7s0 (regression, +0.99%): Moving `reg_file` storage to `distributed_ram` behind a resettable `written_q` mask removes the BRAM read bottleneck but exposes WB-tag forwarding/redirect control, leaving only 0.99% median Fmax gain at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r7s1 (improvement, +9.68%): Banked flop RF storage paired with decode-registered forwarding selectors breaks both RF-origin and live tag-comparison paths into EX at unchanged CoreMark cycles, whereas either change alone leaves the other timing bottleneck.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r8s0 (regression, +5.97%): Sharing branch 