{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('.tmp/agent-logs/impl.hyp-20261001-003-r2s2.log')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines():\n    try:\n        r=json.loads(line)\n    except ValueError:\n        continue\n    item=r.get('item', {})\n    if item.get('type') == 'agent_message':\n        print(item.get('text', ''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"I’ll inspect the pipeline controls and test setup, add the predictor while preserving the divider controls, then run lint, directed tests, formal, and cosimulation within the time budget.\n\nThe predictor is wired in with one-bit metadata, alignment and bounds gating, and EX recovery on a direction mismatch. The divider controls are unchanged. I’m adding directed tests for warmup, loop exits, and stalls; lint found one expected unused-PC-bits warning, which I’ll scope locally before starting formal.\n\nLint now passes. Formal and Gowin timing are running. The cosim build defaults to `NRET=2`, so I restarted it with `NRET=1` to match the unchanged top-level interface.\n\nAll 53 formal checks passed, and cosimulation passed for selftest and canonical CoreMark. Gowin placement option 0 reports 31.167 MHz, with the worst paths still in the divider. The new pipeline tests also pass. I’m extending the tests for saturation, simultaneous lookup/update behavior, and control transfers adjacent to DIV/REM.\n\nThe full unit suite passed, including real and ALTOPS divider modes. The CoreMark marker window is 4,190,281 cycles, with canonical CRCs, valid UART output, and no out-of-bounds accesses. Gowin’s three placement options give a median of 31.759 MHz, which implies about 75.79 iterations/sec from those local measurements.\n\n[implementation_notes.md](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261001-003-r2s2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"complete