{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"The remaining long paths include forwarding tag comparisons. I’m testing whether four match bits can be computed and registered alongside pipeline advance. That should shorten forwarding control without adding an instruction cycle; the key check is keeping those bits aligned through memory stalls and M-unit completion.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r5s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-001-r5s0\ntitle: \\\"Register forwarding tag matches alongside pipeline advance\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 124.78 at median 54.63 MHz and 4378154 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. An unchanged Gowin run reproduces the 24.469 ns\n  register-file-to-divider-seed path and a nearly tied 24.369 ns\n  register-file-to-ALU-to-memory path. Both consume EX operand forwarding.\n  Prior radix-4, operand preservation and separate address-generation\n  micro-optimizations did not beat the later champions. Move forwarding\n  equality work to the existing pipeline edge so several EX consumers\n  benefit from the same local control optimization.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retain the current forwarding data muxes and two-bit select interface,\n  but replace the four combinational EX source/destination comparisons\n  with four registered match bits. Compute those bits on the existing\n  instruction-transfer edge using the source and producer tags that\n  will occupy ID/EX, EX/MEM and MEM/WB immediately after that edge.\n  This adds four control flops, no datapath stage or instruction clock.\n\n  In forward_unit, add clock/reset, the frontend capture condition,\n  EX/MEM hold and MEM/WB hold inputs, the raw fetched source addresses,\n  and the current ID/EX destination. Define next_rs1/next_rs2 as the raw\n  fetched instruction fields when ID captures (\"'!stall_id), otherwise\n  the held ID/EX source addresses. Define next_ex_rd as the held EX/MEM\n  destination during stall_ex_mem, otherwise the current ID/EX rd.\n  Define next_wb_rd as the retained MEM/WB destination during hold_mem_wb,\n  otherwise the current EX/MEM rd. On every non-reset edge register\n  next_ex_rd!=0 && next_ex_rd==next_rs1/rs2 and\n  next_wb_rd!=0 && next_wb_rd==next_rs1/rs2. Reset all four flags to zero.\n  Recompute flags during holds; never merely freeze every flag on a\n  frontend stall, because older instructions can drain while EX is held.\n\n  Qualify these registered comparisons with the CURRENT EX/MEM and\n  MEM/WB valid/reg_write enables already supplied by core.sv. Preserve\n  EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and the existing 00/01/10 encoding. Keep\n  qualification live rather than predicting register-write validity:\n  this preserves suppression for misalignment traps, invalid bubbles\n  and drained M execution slots. When M owns EX and emits an invalid\n  EX/MEM bubble, its speculative next destination comparison is inert\n  because current EX/MEM write enable is false. When M completes, its\n  captured destination equals the held ID/EX destination, so the same\n  next_ex_rd rule applies to the acceptance edge. Keep M metadata and\n  ownership rules unchanged; verify this correspondence explicitly.\n\n  Connect the new inputs in core.sv to the existing stage tags and hold\n  controls. An instruction-memory stall still allows ID to capture raw\n  payload with valid zero when no other interlock holds it. A recovery\n  flush changes validity while preserving the current payload-capture\n  policy. Compute matches for that actual payload even if invalid;\n  current producer enables and pipeline validity enforce side effects.\n  Preserve WB operand refresh in held ID/EX entries, the load-use bubble,\n  zero-register behavior and EX/MEM forwarding eligibility exactly.\n\n  Moving equality comparisons ahead of their consumers should shorten\n  paths from source/destination tags through forwarding selection into\n  branch resolution, ordinary ALU execution and M-unit launch. Preserve\n  all arithmetic, predictor behavior, seven-loop divider timing, bus\n  handshakes, nret=1 and every decoder, RVFI and correctness invariant.\n  At unchanged marker cycles, the acceptance bar needs median Fmax above\n  57.143 MHz and fitness above 130.52. Expected improvement is 12 percent\n  with medium confidence pending vendor timing and unchanged gates.\n  Main risks are misaligned flags during partial stalls and new ID-side\n  next-tag mux paths; require exact forwarding equivalence and the\n  original liveness deadline, rather than changing either contract.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Register four source/destination nonzero equality flags from the\n      actual next pipeline tags, updating them during partial holds.\n      Add clock/reset and tag/hold inputs. Qualify flags with current\n      producer write enables and retain the existing encoded outputs\n      and EX/MEM-first priority; remove current-stage tag comparators.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire raw fetch source fields, current ID/EX destination, clock,\n      reset and existing ID/EX, EX/MEM and MEM/WB advance/hold conditions\n      into forward_unit. Preserve all existing data, hazard and RVFI\n      connections and the declared nret=1 top-level port set.\n  - file: test/test_forward_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Add a sequential forwarding scoreboard against independent current\n      tag comparisons. Exercise capture and every partial-hold pattern,\n      x0, both matching producers, changing write enables, invalid\n      entries and reset. Model actual pipeline tag transitions, allowing\n      invalid EX/MEM bubbles to carry arbitrary tags while write enable\n      is false. Require exact 00/01/10 selections after each edge.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing assertions and add dependencies spanning\n      frontend holds, older stalled loads/stores, held WB refresh and\n      M completion. Cover EX/MEM versus WB priority, load-use bubbles,\n      wrong-path payloads and both source ports with independent bus\n      stalls. Keep real/ALTOPS M arithmetic, formal, full-trace cosim\n      and canonical stalled CoreMark checks as unchanged gates.\n'\"''')\nprint('Updated complete hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
