ve\n  57.143 MHz and fitness above 130.52. Expected improvement is 12 percent\n  with medium confidence pending vendor timing and unchanged gates.\n  Main risks are misaligned flags during partial stalls and new ID-side\n  next-tag mux paths; require exact forwarding equivalence and the\n  original liveness deadline, rather than changing either contract.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Register four source/destination nonzero equality flags from the\n      actual next pipeline tags, updating them during partial holds.\n      Add clock/reset and tag/hold inputs. Qualify flags with current\n      producer write enables and retain the existing encoded outputs\n      and EX/MEM-first priority; remove current-stage tag comparators.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire raw fetch source fields, current ID/EX destination, clock,\n      reset and existing ID/EX, EX/MEM and MEM/WB advance/hold conditions\n      into forward_unit. Preserve all existing data, hazard and RVFI\n      connections and the declared nret=1 top-level port set.\n  - file: test/test_forward_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Add a sequential forwarding scoreboard against independent current\n      tag comparisons. Exercise capture and every partial-hold pattern,\n      x0, both matching producers, changing write enables, invalid\n      entries and reset. Model actual pipeline tag transitions, allowing\n      invalid EX/MEM bubbles to carry arbitrary tags while write enable\n      is false. Require exact 00/01/10 selections after each edge.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing assertions and add dependencies spanning\n      frontend holds, older stalled loads/stores, held WB refresh and\n      M completion. Cover EX/MEM versus WB priority, load-use bubbles,\n      wrong-path payloads and both source ports with independent bus\n      stalls. Keep