{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n  print('\\\\nID:',d['id'],'TITLE:',d['title'])\n  print('fitness:',d.get('fitness'),'fmax:',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles:',d.get('cycles'),'path:',d.get('critical_path'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis','')\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: baseline-bench-4a6c0e6 TITLE: Baseline retest for cores/bench\nfitness: 12.12 fmax: 5.442 cycles: 4491485 path: {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -189.542, 'arrival_ns': 199.837, 'logic_cells': 156, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nHYPOTHESIS: \n\nID: hyp-20261001-001-r1s0 TITLE: Shared radix-4 combinational divider with borrow-based digit selection\nfitness: 13.98 fmax: 6.277 cycles: 4491485 path: {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -156.85, 'arrival_ns': 167.145, 'logic_cells': 452, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nHYPOTHESIS: Replace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with one shared, fully\ncombinational unsigned radix-4 restoring divider. Convert operands to\nunsigned magnitudes for DIV and REM, and use the original bit patterns\nfor DIVU and REMU. Precompute widened divisor multiples B, 2B and 3B.\nUnroll 16 digit stages, consuming the dividend from its most significant\npair of bits to its least significant pair. At each stage form the\nwidened partial dividend P = 4*remainder + next_pair, calculate P-B,\nP-2B and P-3B in parallel, and select the largest nonnegative trial to\nemit quotient digit 0, 1, 2 or 3 and the corresponding new remainder.\nDerive trial validity from each widened subtraction's borrow bit rather\nthan instantiating a separate magnitude comparator followed by another\nsubtraction. Use sufficient unsigned width throughout: divisor\nmultiples and partial dividends require 34 bits, and trial differences\nrequire 35 bits to retain underflow. Expose quotient and remainder from\nthe same network; apply quotient sign from a_sign XOR b_sign and\nremainder sign from a_sign only for signed operations. Explicitly\noverride division by zero before sign correction: quotient is all ones\nand remainder is the original dividend. Preserve INT_MIN/-1 results.\nThis targets fewer serial quotient-selection stages and shared arithmetic\nto\n…[17490 of 20562 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3f35eeed02945f4c]…\nercent acceptance threshold. Do not treat\nthis simple prototype as an accepted improvement.\n\nThe prototype passed the existing ALU tests plus over 160000\ndirected/random shift checks, a combinational SAT proof of complete\nALU equivalence for every op/a/b combination, and selftest plus all\nthree random programs in both ready and independently stalled bus\nmodes. Canonical CoreMark passed all five CRCs, UART and bounds\nvalidation with markers at 15235 and 4393389, preserving exactly\n4378154 measured cycles. These are scratch-prototype checks; full\nunchanged riscv-formal remains a required implementation gate.\n\nAt unchanged 4378154 marker cycles the acceptance bar requires median\nFmax above 61.78 MHz and fitness above 141.11. Main risks are synthesis\nmapping differences and branch-training or divider paths becoming\nlimiting. Require a fresh unchanged evaluator run, all formal checks,\nstalled full-trace cosim, canonical CoreMark CRCs, UART validation,\nbounds and marker-window checks on the implemented candidate.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
