{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"agent_message","text":"The LUT-RAM register-file prototype reached 67.925 MHz on placement option 0, versus 61.919 MHz for the reproduced champion. It replaced two register-file BRAMs with 32 LUT-RAMs. Reset now clears per-register validity bits, so unwritten registers still read zero. I’ll check the other placements and correctness before making this the final hypothesis."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r7s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-001-r7s0',\n'title':'LUT-RAM register file with exact logical reset via written bits',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 146.62 at median 64.191 MHz with 4378154 CoreMark marker cycles. Its worst Gowin path starts at a register-file BRAM output, passes through ID and operand forwarding, and reaches fetch-PC control. Reproducing placement option 0 gives exactly the recorded 61.919 MHz and 21.444 ns arrival. The register-file BRAM clock-to-output contribution is 2.260 ns. Prior preservation of ID/EX flops failed to improve fitness, and shift sharing and BHT cleanup fell below the 4.6 percent threshold. Changing the actual register storage mapping can remove that slow BRAM output while preserving architectural timing.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace only the register-file storage implementation with asynchronous distributed LUT RAM plus a per-register written bitmap. Declare the 32x32 data array with Gowin's supported syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute. Remove reset assignments from the data array; its only update is a synchronous write when \"'!reset && w_en && w_addr!=0. Reset a 32-bit written_q bitmap to zero. On each non-reset valid nonzero write, set written_q[w_addr] to one. Maintain the existing write-first read priority on each port: address zero returns zero; a matching current w_en write returns w_data; otherwise return regs[read_addr] only if written_q[read_addr] is set, and zero if it is clear.\n\nThis gives exactly the existing synchronous reset behavior even after dirtying every register and on repeated reset: stale or uninitialized LUT-RAM contents never become visible. A simultaneous reset and write clears written_q and does not update storage. Preserve the original combinational bypass even while reset is asserted, because reset affects stored architectural state only at the rising edge. Writes to x0 never set a validity bit or modify a readable register. Use nonblocking updates so the bitmap and data become visible together on the same edge. Do not serialize reset or add an initialization sweep, read clock, operand capture edge or bypass pipeline stage.\n\nKeep the reg_file module interface, both combinational read ports, single synchronous write port and every pipeline control and instruction latency. Leave ID/EX capture and WB refresh, EX forwarding priority, load-use protection, predictor, memory requests, M arithmetic/ALTOPS lifecycle, nret=1, RVFI and all invariants unchanged. This is one micro-optimization of register-file inference and logical reset; it does not change prediction or memory caching.\n\nAn isolated prototype of exactly this change reached 67.925 MHz on Gowin placement option 0 versus the reproduced champion'\"'s 61.919 MHz, a 9.70 percent gain on that option. It mapped to 4336 logic cells, 1135 registers, 32 LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs versus 4189, 1156, eight LUT RAMs, six BSRAMs and two DSPs. The critical path moved away from register-file BRAM output to writeback-tag control, with 20.016 ns arrival. These are prototype results, not an accepted score; evaluate all three scored placement options and preserve the unchanged correctness gates.\n\nAt the same marker count, acceptance needs median Fmax above 67.144 MHz and fitness above 153.365 iterations/second. Expected fitness gain is 8 percent with medium confidence pending the placement median and correctness checks. Main risks are validity/read gating, duplicated two-port RAM routing, and WB-tag or divider paths limiting the gain. Require that synthesis actually maps the data array into LUT RAM: syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed\\\" is invalid in this Gowin release, while \\\"distributed_ram\\\" produced the measured mapping. Do not use vendor primitives that bypass portable simulation/formal.\n\nVerify both read ports against an independent architectural register model, including write-first bypass, x0, all 31 writable addresses, initial reset, dirty repeated resets, reset/write collisions and independent addresses. Retain all original tests and formal/liveness assertions. Require ready and independently stalled full-trace cosim, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds validation and identical marker cycles on the implemented candidate. Full formal remains mandatory in the implementation phase.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Use a portable unreset 32x32 data array with syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed_ram\\\" and a synchronously reset written_q bitmap. Write data and mark its address only on non-reset nonzero writes. Preserve x0 and same-cycle write-first priority; mask unwritten stored values to zero on both asynchronous read ports. Keep the external module interface and architectural read/write timing.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain every existing test and add independent reference-model checks for both read ports, all writable registers, dirty repeated reset, simultaneous reset/write, first writes after reset, x0, matching-port write bypass and unrelated-port reads. Add deterministic randomized read/write/reset sequences without relying on internal RAM contents. Run existing pipeline, core M, formal and stalled cosim/CoreMark gates unchanged.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88,default_style=None))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nr=Path('cores/bench/generated/r7_micro_probe')\n(r/'lutrf2_other_options.py').write_text('''import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build\nwt=Path.cwd();p=wt/'cores/bench/generated/r7_micro_probe'\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n fs={pool.submit(build,wt,p/'lutrf2_rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',option,p/('lutrf2_p'+str(option))):option for option in [1,2]}\n for f in as_completed(fs):\n  option=fs[f];r=f.result();(p/('lutrf2_p'+str(option)+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2));print(json.dumps(r),flush=True)\n''')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
