{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r9s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-001-r9s0\ntitle: \"Retimed BHT training writes with exact pending-event lookup bypass\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion achieves 174.65 iterations/second at 76.466 MHz. Its\n  reported worst path runs from MEM/WB load data through forwarded\n  branch resolution into an IF BHT counter register, with 18.933 ns\n  arrival. Registering recovery predicates already shortened redirect\n  logic, but late branch training still drives the variable-index\n  counter read/modify/write and its distributed write enables. Earlier\n  banked local-counter feedback alone improved fitness only 1.27 percent;\n  it did not insert a timing boundary between resolution and updating.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming the existing BHT training transaction should break this\n  measured path without changing architectural clocks or even the\n  prediction sequence. Inside if_stage, capture each incoming EX\n  training event in a one-entry pipeline register containing valid,\n  the existing six-bit folded index and taken. Apply the previous\n  registered event to the physical 64-entry two-bit counter array on\n  the next rising edge. This is an always-draining pipeline stage,\n  not a queue with backpressure: it accepts a fresh event every cycle.\n\n  Preserve the original predictor's logical state with a combinational\n  lookup bypass. Let C be the physical counter at fetch_index and\n  P the pending registered event. The effective lookup counter is\n  sat_step(C, P.taken) when P.valid and P.index == fetch_index;\n  otherwise it is C. sat_step increments toward 3 for taken and\n  decrements toward 0 for not-taken. Use this effective counter's\n  high bit for the existing branch-direction decision. The physical\n  write uses sat_step(counters_q[P.index], P.taken), not the bypassed\n  value, so a training event is never applied twice.\n\n  Nonblocking edge semantics are essential: each edge commits the old\n  pending event to physical storage and independently captures the\n  new EX event. Before the edge, fetch sees exactly the original\n  table afte\n…[1938 of 5010 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e7a9df2de2b0599c]…\n_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add preserved pending training valid/index/taken registers, commit\n      their previous event to the existing counter table, and bypass\n      exactly that pending saturating update into fetch lookup. Preserve\n      the public internal IF interface and every original prediction,\n      reset, target, hold and redirect behavior. Do not alter EX event\n      qualification or add frontend backpressure.\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Keep all existing assertions. Add an independent immediate-update\n      BHT reference and compare each cycle's effective prediction with\n      back-to-back aliased training, opposite outcomes, both saturation\n      boundaries, event gaps, random fetch/train indices, stalls,\n      redirects and reset with a pending event. Verify event commit\n      and capture never duplicate or lose an update and require the\n      unchanged pipeline, formal and canonical CoreMark gates.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
