{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r10s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-002-r10s1\ntitle: \\\"Four-entry shift-register fetch queue with fall-through decode\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.65 at 76.466 MHz with 4378154 CoreMark marker\n  cycles. Fetch stops during load-use, data-memory and M execution\n  holds, wasting opportunities to hide the approximately 22 percent\n  independent instruction-bus stalls. Prior structural r9s1 reduced\n  cycles to 4095164 with two queue entries, but its 72.864 MHz median\n  limited improvement to 1.88 percent. This supports decoupling while\n  motivating additional depth and a simpler fixed-head topology.\n  Four fixed flop slots can cover longer downstream holds without\n  RAM inference or a circular-head address mux. Isolated experiments\n  with larger divider networks lost frequency through routing growth;\n  buffering has the stronger measured fitness result.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decouple physical fetch from decode using exactly four flop-based\n  shift-register slots, three-bit occupancy 0..4, and empty-queue\n  fall-through. This is one structural frontend-buffering change.\n  Preserve the execution pipeline, arithmetic units and predictor.\n\n  Slots contain existing if_id_t: full PC, raw instruction, accepted\n  prediction and validity. Keep a separate physical fetch PC driving\n  io_imemAddr. Present fixed queue[0] when nonempty, otherwise the\n  incoming physical PC/instruction/prediction. Expose head_available\n  independently of flush as occupancy\"'!=0 OR io_imemReady; head valid\n  additionally requires no reset, flush or EX recovery. This prevents\n  availability from feeding itself through hazard-driven flush logic.\n\n  Physical acceptance is io_imemReady AND occupancy<4, qualified by\n  no reset/recovery. Head consumption requires head_available and\n  no downstream hold. An incoming instruction consumed through empty\n  fall-through advances fetch without entering storage. Otherwise an\n  accepted fetch appends at occupancy minus any simultaneous queued\n  pop. A queued pop shifts slots 1..3 toward fixed slot zero; a\n  simultaneous append overrides the vacated tail. Occupancy changes\n  by stored_push minus queued_pop. Distinguish fall-through consumption\n  from storage dequeue. Use compile-time fixed slot assignments with\n  decoded append enables; do not infer variable-index storage, RAM\n  or a circular head mux. When full, decline physical fetch for that\n  edge even if a queued head pops, keeping fetch admission dependent\n  on registered occupancy alone. Head issue still proceeds. States\n  zero through three sustain simultaneous fetch and issue when allowed.\n\n  Advance fetch on physical acceptance during load-use, data-memory\n  and M holds, filling available slots. Never advance or enqueue while\n  io_imemReady=0. Buffered heads can issue during physical bus stalls.\n  RF read addresses and load-use tags come from the current queue head,\n  and hazard instruction availability comes from head_available.\n  Decode/RF reads and registered forwarding choices occur on actual\n  head consumption; do not prefetch operand values or forwarding tags.\n  Preserve existing data/M holds, held WB refresh and load-use bubbles.\n\n  EX recovery overrides all frontend holds and physical bus stalls.\n  On the existing recovery edge, discard all queued and incoming\n  wrong-path tokens, clear occupancy, invalidate younger ID/EX and\n  set physical fetch PC to the existing correction target. Empty\n  fall-through presents a ready target on the following cycle: no\n  extra redirect register or recovery clock. Reset restores PC zero\n  and empty state. Reproduce the measured prototype'\"'s synchronous\n  clearing of all four slot payloads on reset/recovery; shift/append\n  updates occur only otherwise, with occupancy determining issue.\n  Wrong-path stores and M operations cannot launch or issue.\n\n  Preserve the 64 folded-PC two-bit counters, reset/index/saturation,\n  direct-JAL guards, sequential JALR prediction and exact-once EX\n  training. Lookup uses physical fetch PC, but queued predictions\n  remain snapshots of accepted lookup decisions. Earlier lookup may\n  see different counter versions; do not introduce a new predictor\n  algorithm. Preserve target alignment faults, links, held operands,\n  memory request stability, retirement ordering and nret=1 public IO.\n  Real and ALTOPS builds must retain nine-clock adjacent-DIV and\n  ten-clock unpredicted-transfer-to-DIV progress bounds.\n\n  An isolated prototype of this exact four-slot topology, with all\n  other champion RTL unchanged, measured Gowin options 0/1/2 at\n  70.457/75.662/75.662 MHz, median 75.662 MHz, with 6753 logic cells\n  and 2433 registers. The unchanged canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration\n  CoreMark binary with both stalls passed all expected CRCs, UART\n  success text, bounded accesses and timing-marker validation.\n  Start/stop were 14186/4041395: 4027209 marker cycles, an 8.016\n  percent cycle reduction. Computed fitness is 187.877 iterations/sec,\n  7.573 percent above the champion, clearing the 4.6 percent margin.\n  These are proposal-stage measurements, not evaluator acceptance.\n  The worst option-0 path traverses occupancy, downstream holds and\n  slot-update enables; reproduce the specified topology and rerun\n  all three vendor placements after implementation.\n\n  Require independent FIFO/token-conservation tests across every\n  occupancy, empty fall-through, full admission, simultaneous shift/\n  append, separate and overlapping bus stalls, both recovery directions,\n  stalled target fetches, M/load dependencies, reset and wrong-path\n  effects. Retain every existing architectural assertion and formal\n  bound, full-trace cosim and canonical CoreMark checks. Internal tests\n  identifying physical fetch PC with decode PC must track physical\n  acceptance and ordered head consumption separately, replacing obsolete\n  topology assumptions with an independent FIFO model without weakening\n  architectural expectations. At 4027209 marker cycles, acceptance\n  requires median Fmax above approximately 73.57 MHz, versus the\n  prototype's 75.662 MHz. Remaining risks are implementation differences,\n  queue-control routing and symbolic recovery/stall corner cases.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add four fixed shift-register fetch slots, three-bit occupancy,\n      empty fall-through and explicit physical-accept/head-consume\n      events. Present fixed slot zero, decode append enables, and\n      capture accepted prediction snapshots. Preserve predictor logic\n      and give reset/EX recovery atomic queue-discard and fetch-PC priority.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire physical io_imemReady into the frontend separately from its\n      squash-independent head_available output. Feed hazard availability\n      and load-use source tags from the presented head. Preserve ID/RF\n      head reads, downstream holds/forwarding, recovery/training wiring,\n      single-channel retirement and every public port.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Clarify the internal instruction-availability input as queue-head\n      availability. Apply it to unavailable-head bubbling and load-use\n      qualification while preserving all downstream data/M holds,\n      load-use bubble and recovery priorities.\n  - file: test/test_fetch_queue.py\n    description: |\n      Add an independent four-entry FIFO/prediction model checking token\n      conservation, head ordering, occupancies 0..4, empty fall-through,\n      full admission, all push/pop combinations, stable held heads,\n      buffered issue during bus stalls, reset and atomic recovery.\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt the internal frontend interface while retaining every\n      counter, target-guard, training and recovery assertion. Distinguish\n      physical lookup/accept from queued-head issue; verify stored PC\n      and prediction alignment and recovery during target-fetch stalls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Replace same-PC physical-fetch/decode assumptions with an independent\n      ordered frontend model, retaining every RVFI, legality, operand,\n      memory and retirement assertion. Cover full-queue recovery, queued\n      load-use, overlapping stalls, both prediction-error directions,\n      wrong-path store/M suppression and reset.\n  - file: test/test_core_div.py\n    description: |\n      Track physical fetch separately from head consumption while\n      retaining exact arithmetic/dependencies, held memory/M payloads,\n      exact-once launch/training/retirement and all fixed nine-/ten-clock\n      ready-bus progress bounds. Exercise full queues around control\n      transfers, mixed M operations, separate bus stalls and reset.\n''')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r10s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nprint('Four-entry fetch hypothesis written and schema validated')\nprint('cycle reduction%',(1-4027209/4378154)*100,'required MHz',174.65*1.046*4027209/10/1e6)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
