, full-trace cosim and canonical CoreMark checks. Internal tests\n  identifying physical fetch PC with decode PC must track physical\n  acceptance and ordered head consumption separately, replacing obsolete\n  topology assumptions with an independent FIFO model without weakening\n  architectural expectations. At 4027209 marker cycles, acceptance\n  requires median Fmax above approximately 73.57 MHz, versus the\n  prototype's 75.662 MHz. Remaining risks are implementation differences,\n  queue-control routing and symbolic recovery/stall corner cases.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add four fixed shift-register fetch slots, three-bit occupancy,\n      empty fall-through and explicit physical-accept/head-consume\n      events. Present fixed slot zero, decode append enables, and\n      capture accepted prediction snapshots. Preserve predictor logic\n      and give reset/EX recovery atomic queue-discard and fetch-PC priority.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire physical io_imemReady into the frontend separately from its\n      squash-independent head_available output. Feed hazard availability\n      and load-use source tags from the presented head. Preserve ID/RF\n      head reads, downstream holds/forwarding, recovery/training wiring,\n      single-channel retirement and every public port.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Clarify the internal instruction-availability input as queue-head\n      availability. Apply it to unavailable-head bubbling and load-use\n      qualification while preserving all downstream data/M holds,\n      load-use bubble and recovery priorities.\n  - file: test/test_fetch_queue.py\n    description: |\n      Add an independent four-entry FIFO/prediction model checking token\n      conservation, head ordering, occupancies 0..4, empty fall-through,\n      full admission, all push/pop combinations, stable held heads,\n      buffered issue during bus stalls, reset and atomic recovery.\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt the internal frontend interface while retaining every\n      counter, target-guard, training and recovery assertion. Distinguish\n      physical lookup/accept from queued-head issue; verify stored PC\n      and prediction alignment and recovery during target-fetch stalls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Replace same-PC physical-fetch/decode assumptions with an independent\n      ordered frontend model, retaining every RVFI, legality, operand,\n      memory and retirement assertion. Cover full-queue recovery, queued\n      load-use, overlapping stalls, both prediction-error directions,\n      wrong-path store/M suppression and reset.\n  - file: test/test_core_div.py\n    description: |\n      Track physical fetch separately from head consumption while\n      retaining exact arithmetic/dependencies, held memory/M payloads,\n      exact-once launch/training/retirement and all fixed nine-/ten-clock\n      ready-bus progress bounds. Exercise full queues around control\n      transfers, mixed M operations, separate bus stalls and reset.\n''')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r10s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nprint('Four-entry fetch hypothesis written and schema validated')\nprint('cycle reduction%',(1-4027209/4378154)*100,'required MHz',174.65*1.046*4027209/10/1e6)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Four-entry fetch hypothesis written and schema validated\ncycle reduction% 8.015821279927572 required MHz 73.57062462351001\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
