{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +462.46%): Replacing ALU `/` and `%` with four registered groups of eight restoring steps and separate magnitude/sign stages shortens the critical path enough to outweigh six-cycle DIV/REM latency in CoreMark's division-light timed kernels.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +11.47%): A 64-entry folded-PC BHT plus direct JAL prediction raised CoreMark throughput 7.19% by avoiding EX flush bubbles, but the forwarded combinational DIV/REM path still limited Fmax to 5.66 MHz, making divider pipelining the higher-impact change.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (improvement, +54.58%): Reducing div_unit's serial restoring steps per register from eight to five improves timing without increasing CoreMark's marker cycles, while folding magnitude/sign work into launch/accept edges preserves the ten-clock branch-to-DIV liveness bound despite seven LOOP clocks.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +11.18%): A 64-entry BHT/JAL predictor with one-bit direction recovery removes CoreMark flush bubbles while retaining divider-limited timing, but eight serial restoring steps per divider clock leave Fmax at 31.76 MHz versus the five-step divider's 47.33 MHz.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 (improvement, +6.93%): Pairing a 64-entry BHT/JAL predictor with validity-only ID/EX recovery removes correct-prediction flush bubbles without routing EX redirects through instruction masking or wide payload enables, making prediction profitable on the five-step divider.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r4s1 (improvement, +10.74%): With MUL and DIV tied for the critical path, registering MUL limb products and carry-save reduction while seeding four DIV bits at launch shortens both arithmetic cones enough to offset CoreMark's two extra MUL clocks without lengthening