{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r12s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Circular fetch buffering with dequeue-independent payload writes\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 182.73 at 80.002 MHz and 4378154 CoreMark marker\n  cycles. Earlier two- and four-entry fall-through fetch buffers reduced\n  marker cycles to 4095164 and 4027209 respectively, but lost enough Fmax\n  to miss acceptance. r10s1 used a shifting queue whose wide payload\n  update enables depended on downstream hazard/recovery control. A\n  writeback-boundary prototype for this round passed existing pipeline\n  and real/ALTOPS M tests but measured only 77.105 MHz median, exposing\n  the divider and EX operand selection. The most promising structural\n  opportunity is therefore to retain demonstrated fetch-stall hiding\n  while changing buffer storage so dequeue never updates wide payloads.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a four-slot circular fall-through fetch buffer between the\n  physical fetch/predictor cursor and decode. Each fixed 65-bit slot\n  stores accepted PC, instruction and prediction direction. Use a\n  two-bit read pointer, two-bit write pointer and three-bit occupancy;\n  select the queued head from fixed slot registers. Never shift entries\n  or move their payloads on consumption. Empty fall-through preserves\n  the current ready-bus fetch-to-ID latency, and physical fetching can\n  continue while decode waits for load-use, data memory or M execution.\n  This is one queue-topology change, with unchanged predictor capacity,\n  arithmetic, recovery stage and single retirement channel.\n\n  Let physical_accept = io_imemReady && occupancy < 4 && !reset.\n  Deliberately use registered occupancy alone for admission: do not\n  make a full queue's same-edge admission depend on downstream consume.\n  Let head_available = occupancy != 0 || io_imemReady, independently of\n  squash, and consume = head_available && !decode_hold && !redirect\n  && !reset. An empty accepted fetch consumed immediately advances both\n  pointers with unchanged zero occupancy. An accepted unconsumed fetch\n  writes t\n…[4643 of 7715 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:93e9e7204d9ec2a1]…\n  description: |\n      Adapt the internal frontend interface while retaining every\n      legality, counter, alias, target-guard, training and recovery check.\n      Distinguish physical acceptance from stored-head consumption and\n      verify captured PC/prediction alignment through queue wraparound.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Replace physical-fetch-equals-decode assumptions with an independent\n      queued-token model, retaining every legality, operand, memory and\n      RVFI assertion. Cover queued load-use, combined stalls, both branch\n      correction directions, forwarded JALR, full-queue recovery and\n      suppression of wrong-path stores and M launches.\n  - file: test/test_core_div.py\n    description: |\n      Track physical fetch separately from head consumption while\n      retaining exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, held forwarding, memory/M\n      stability, exact-once events and fixed nine-/ten-clock progress\n      bounds. Exercise mixed M/load streams, full queues and reset.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
