{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor r in map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r9s1','r10s1','r11s0','r12s0','r10s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r.get('fitness'),r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('cycles'),r.get('critical_path'))\n  h=r['hypothesis']\n  print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-002-r9s1 Decouple fetch with a two-entry fall-through instruction queue 177.93 72.864 4095164 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_m/u_div/remainder_q_0_s1', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_m/u_div/remainder_q_1_s1', 'slack_ns': -8.414, 'arrival_ns': 18.708, 'logic_cells': 71, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_m/u_div']}\nIntroduce a two-entry, flop-based, fall-through instruction queue\nbetween the existing predicted fetch-PC engine and decode. This is\none structural frontend decoupling change: preserve single-issue EX,\nthe existing predictor algorithm and arithmetic/pipeline boundaries,\nwhile using otherwise idle fetch opportunities to hide part of the\napproximately 22 percent instruction-bus backpressure.\n\nEach queue entry contains the existing if_id_t payload: full 32-bit\nPC and instruction, the prediction actually used to advance fetch,\nand validity. Keep a separate fetch PC driving io_imemAddr. When\nthe queue is empty, expose the current bus payload directly to ID;\na ready fetch consumed by ID on that edge advances fetch without\nentering storage. If decode is blocked, capture that ready payload\nand advance the fetch PC anyway. With queued entries, ID sees the\noldest entry, independently of the current bus instruction/ready.\nFetch into free storage during load-use, dmem and M holds until the\nqueue fills. Never advance or enqueue on io_imemReady=0. Buffered\npayload and its accepted prediction remain stable until consumed\nor squashed