{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"The pipelined-MUL prototype clears the threshold: it uses 4,002,936 CoreMark marker cycles and reaches 81.940 MHz median Fmax, implying 204.70 iterations/sec—a 5.82% gain. Canonical CRC/UART/bounds checks and eight ready/stalled ISS cosims pass. I’m updating the hypothesis file around this change."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-002-r13s1',\n'title':'Pipeline multiply through EX/MEM and MEM/WB with a late-result interlock',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 193.44 at 79.218 MHz with 4095164 timed CoreMark\ncycles. Earlier structural attempts successfully registered DSP limb\nproducts and carry-save reduction, but multiplication still owns EX\nthrough launch/reduce/accept and stalls every younger instruction for\ntwo extra clocks. A stalled full-program retirement trace contains\n94054 MUL and ten MULHU instructions; timed matrix multiplication makes\nthis an opportunity to improve throughput without another fetch stage.\nThe existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB boundaries can hold those two arithmetic\nsteps while an ordinary younger instruction advances behind a multiply.\nThis retains the arithmetic partition that previously improved timing.\nThe first fetch-successor retiming prototypes in this round did not\nclear the acceptance margin; their changes are not part of this proposal.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Move all four multiplication operations out of the EX-owned busy/done\ntransaction and into a fully pipelined execution lane spanning the\nexisting EX/MEM and MEM/WB boundaries. Keep division and remainder as\nEX-owned operations with the current raw launch/PREP/six-LOOP/accept\nschedule. This is one structural repartition of multiplication and its\ndependency interlock, with the same single-issue pipeline, fetch queue,\nbranch predictor, integer datapath and single retirement channel.\n\nAt each actual EX advance, capture the four existing 16-bit limb\nproducts alongside that instruction's EX/MEM metadata. Use unsigned\nlow limbs and signed 17-bit high limbs with exactly the present\nMULH/MULHU/MULHSU sign rules: 32-bit low-low, signed 34-bit low-high,\nhigh-low and high-high products. Preserve full intermediate widths.\nThese must be real EX/MEM register boundaries, including DSP output\nregisters where inferred; do not synthesize a combinational full-width\nmultiply from EX operands to MEM/WB. Carry an is_mul bit with the token.\nIn MEM form the existing four shifted/sign-extended 64-bit terms, use\ntwo carry-save levels and one final carry-propagating add, and capture\nthe selected low/high 32-bit product in MEM/WB's scalar-result field on\nits normal acceptance edge. There is no separate product-DONE register,\nEX/MEM completion arbitration, multiply metadata transaction or\nfrontend busy interval. An independent instruction can follow a MUL on\nthe next EX edge; consecutive independent multiplies sustain one per\nclock when the frontend and memory stages can advance.\n\nA MUL result is available only in WB, so treat a validated multiply in\nEX as a late producer just like the existing load for immediate source\ndependencies. Insert exactly one bubble when a nonzero multiply rd\nmatches either presented fetch-head source, retaining the existing\nconservative raw-source matching. Unrelated instructions and writes to\nx0 incur no multiply hold. Never advertise an advancing MUL as an\nEX/MEM scalar forwarding producer: its ordinary alu_result is not the\nproduct. The current MEM MUL is a valid producer of the next MEM/WB\nvalue and uses the existing registered selector 10. Preserve younger\nwriter precedence and selector maintenance/held-WB operand refresh.\nForward completed products to ALU, memory address/data, branch/JALR\nand DIV operands through the registered WB result, not through a new\ncombinational MEM-to-EX multiplier bypass. Keep ordinary ALU and load\nforwarding behavior and division producer acceptance unchanged.\n\nData backpressure holds the complete EX/MEM token, every partial\nproduct and its control/metadata together. The younger EX MUL captures\nnothing until EX can advance. MEM/WB retains its data and clears valid\non a stall as today, preventing duplicate retirement. On release the\nproduct enqueues exactly once. Squashed/invalid/illegal entries cannot\nwrite architectural state, redirect, train or issue memory. Division\nstart/accept still captures operands and metadata once, drains older\ninstructions and holds completion across an older memory stall; older\npipeline MULs may drain during DIV execution. Reset cancels division,\nclears multiplication token validity and restores pipeline state.\nMultiplication uses the same in-order retirement edge as an ordinary\nALU instruction, with its original instruction, forwarded sources,\nPC/link metadata and zero memory masks. Retain nret=1 and every public\nport, default-illegal and EBREAK-only SYSTEM policies, traps, bounds,\nstrict RVFI order and all unchanged correctness contracts.\n\nIn RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS capture each exact existing MUL-family substitute\nat EX/MEM and pass it through MEM/WB with the same token acceptance and\nlate-result dependency policy as the real arithmetic. Leave DIV/REM\nsubstitution formulas and latency intact. Do not weaken arithmetic\nvectors or formal progress bounds. Ready-bus adjacent DIV retirements\nremain nine clocks apart and recovered-transfer-to-DIV progress stays\nwithin ten. Replace obsolete tests of EX-owned MUL start/busy/completion\nwith independent pipeline token/operand/result checks and stronger\nassertions of one-cycle independent MUL retirement spacing and the\nsingle bubble for immediate consumers. Keep every architectural result,\ntrap, request-stability and exact-once retirement assertion.\n\nAn isolated prototype with only this multiplication repartition measured\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 at 77.345/81.940/81.940 MHz, median 81.940,\nwith 6689 LUT4, 2283 FF, four BSRAMs, no LUT RAM and two DSPs. Canonical\nstalled 2K/-O3/10-iteration CoreMark passed every CRC, required UART text,\nbounded accesses and marker validation. Start/stop markers were\n14326/4017262: 4002936 measured cycles, 2.252 percent fewer than the\nchampion. Computed fitness is 204.69975 iterations/sec, 5.8208 percent\nabove 193.44, exceeding the 4.6 percent margin. Selftest and all three\nexisting randomized programs passed full-trace ISS cosim with ready\nand independently stalled buses, eight runs. These are proposal-stage\nmeasurements; full formal and revised pipeline/M cocotb verification\nremain requirements for implementation, not claimed passes.\n\nReproduce the two arithmetic boundaries and interlock, rather than\ncombining this with unrelated fetch/ALU/divider optimizations. Require\nfresh median vendor timing and canonical CoreMark gates; at 4002936\ncycles acceptance requires median Fmax above 80.994703 MHz. The measured\nmargin is modest, so confidence is medium. Main risks are forwarding\neligibility for the late producer, stale partials across data stalls,\nback-to-back writer ordering, altered synthesis of DSP registers and\nrouting variance. Add directed streams covering every multiply form,\nsigned extrema, low/high products, both dependent sources, x0, competing\nwriters, MUL-to-load/store/branch/JALR/DIV, DIV-to-MUL, arbitrary bus\nstalls, older stalled memory, reset and wrong-path multiplication.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Extend the EX/MEM token with an is_mul flag and the full-width registered\nlimb products (32-bit LL and signed 34-bit LH/HL/HH), preserving the\nexisting metadata fields and public interface. Keep MUL control and\npartials aligned through bubbles, stalls and reset.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Launch pipelined MUL-family limb products into EX/MEM on actual ordinary\nEX advance. Restrict EX-owned start/busy/done handling to DIV/REM, using\ndiv_unit directly or an equivalently division-only integration so no\nduplicate timed multiplier remains. Exclude MUL from EX/MEM scalar\nproducer eligibility; preserve DIV acceptance, metadata, forwarding,\nrequest formation and redirects. Capture exact ALTOPS MUL substitutes\nat the same boundary and keep all multiply fields frozen on data holds.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Reduce each advancing MUL token's registered limbs with the existing\ntwo-level carry-save network and final 64-bit add. Select the RV32M\nlow/high result into the existing MEM/WB scalar-result register, using\nthe carried opcode. Preserve load extraction, memory outputs, data hold\nand exact-once retirement; ALTOPS uses the captured substitute result.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Qualify EX-stage MUL-family tokens as late-result hazards alongside\nloads. Retain the registered forwarding topology: no EX scalar MUL\nproducer, but current MEM MUL forwards its next WB result. Keep nret=1,\nall external ports and retirement wiring intact.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Generalize the existing load-use condition to load-or-multiply late\nresults without adding a second interlock or broad frontend hold.\nPreserve one-bubble dependency handling, nonzero destinations, buffered\nhead source tags and priority of data/DIV holds and same-cycle recovery.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Keep every architectural assertion and adapt only obsolete EX-owned\nMUL lifecycle/internal bundle monitors to pipelined tokens. Add independent\nMUL streams with one-per-clock retirement spacing, immediate dependent\nconsumers requiring one bubble, and competing writers under bus stalls.\nCover products feeding memory requests, branches and JALR.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_core_div.py','description':'''Retain exact results for all real/ALTOPS M operations and unchanged\nDIV liveness/held-completion checks. Model pipelined multiplication\ninstead of requiring MUL start/busy events or three-clock retirement\ngaps. Verify MUL/DIV mixtures, both source dependencies, sign/high-half\ncases, reset, older data holds and ordered exact-once retirement.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain all exact arithmetic vectors. Extend real multiply verification\nto the core's pipelined EX/MEM-to-MEM/WB path so the actual used hardware,\nincluding all signed high-half modes, remains covered after standalone\nm_unit multiplication is no longer the core execution route.\n'''}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote schema-valid structural hypothesis',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
