{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_45", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, yaml\nfrom jsonschema import validate\np = Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r2s1.yaml')\nh = yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['motivation'] = '''The structural champion hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 improved fitness to\n68.17 by removing combinational division from the ordinary ALU.\nIts measured worst path now lies entirely inside u_ex/u_div, from\nremainder_q to remainder_q: 38.355 ns, 252 cells and 48 logic levels,\nwith median Fmax 30.617 MHz. Eight serial restoring subtract/select\nsteps still occupy each registered iteration. The previous division\nlatency change left CoreMark's marker-window count at 4491485 cycles.\nRepartitioning the remaining divider chain is the strongest structural\nopportunity in this history: it directly attacks the measured critical\npath, while prior predictor work improved throughput by 7.19 percent.\n'''\nh['hypothesis'] = '''Rebalance only the registered division execution topology. Replace\nthe current PREP -> four eight-bit LOOP clocks -> CORRECT sequence\nwith magnitude capture on the accepted launch edge, seven five-bit\nLOOP clocks, and sign/exception selection on the DONE-to-EX/MEM\nacceptance path. Each LOOP clock performs exactly five existing\nunsigned restoring trial subtract/select steps. Keep radix-2\narithmetic and the shared quotient/remainder recurrence; the change\nmoves arithmetic across register boundaries and reduces the maximum\nserial quotient-update depth from eight steps to five.\n\nOn start && \"'!busy, compute 32-bit unsigned operand magnitudes for\nDIV/REM and capture divisor, original dividend, operation, sign flags\nand exceptional-case flags. Load a 35-bit quotient shift register\nwith {3'\"'b0, magnitude_dividend}; zero the remainder and three-bit loop\ncounter. Seven uniform five-bit groups consume all 35 bits. The three\nleading zeros do not change the mathematical result for a nonzero\ndivisor. Read the final quotient from its low 32 bits. Preserve the\nexisting explicit divisor-zero and INT_MIN/-1 overrides.\n\nAfter the seventh LOOP update, freeze the raw quotient, remainder\nand request metadata in DONE. Form result combinationally from these\nfrozen registers, applying quotient sign, dividend-signed remainder\nand exceptional-case selection. The existing EX/MEM register captures\nthat corrected result on accept. Do not add a separate correction\nclock or put sign correction on a LOOP subtraction path. Result must\nremain stable throughout DONE until accepted, including arbitrary\nolder data-memory stalls. Reset cancels the outstanding request.\n\nPreserve the div_unit start/busy/done/accept interface and current\nEX ownership, metadata capture, load dependency protection and held\nsource refresh. Older instructions drain while EX divides, and each\ndivision enqueues and retires exactly once. The accepted launch edge\nis followed by seven LOOP edges and one EX/MEM acceptance edge; the\nnext adjacent division can launch on the following edge. Ready-bus\nretirement spacing is nine clocks for adjacent divisions and ten\nclocks for a taken branch/JAL/JALR immediately followed by division.\nThe latter includes the existing frontend recovery bubble and is\nwhy an eight-LOOP design is unsuitable for the unchanged ten-clock\nformal liveness deadline. Preserve this bound for every symbolic\nstream without changing formal configuration, nret=1 or public IO.\n\nKeep the exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS DIV/REM formulas and the same\nseven-iteration lifecycle in that build. Preserve all arithmetic,\nordered-retirement, decoder, memory and CoreMark correctness checks.\nIn the core test driver, scope remembered held-result values to one\ncontiguous DONE interval: clear the remembered sample when DONE is\nlow, then retain the existing equality assertion for all stalled\nsamples of that request. The old helper retained one instruction's\nsample across later completed divisions, causing a false failure\nwhen the changed latency moved a later result onto a random stall.\nCheck stable completion per request; never compare unrelated results.\n\nA scratch prototype of exactly this topology, using unchanged EX,\nhazard and ordinary ALU RTL, measured Gowin Fmax 43.059/45.991/45.991\nMHz for placement options 0/1/2: median 45.991 MHz, 2871 logic cells\nand 651 registers. Stalled CoreMark passed all five canonical CRCs,\nthe required UART literal and bounds checks, and retained the exact\n4491485-cycle MMIO marker window. Computed prototype fitness is\n102.396, about 50.2 percent above 68.17. This is proposal-stage evidence,\nnot an orchestrator acceptance result. Real and ALTOPS arithmetic\nvectors, reset/handshake tests, extended core dependency/stall tests,\nselftest cosim with ready and stalled buses, and the unchanged\nliveness/unique/DIV/REM formal tasks passed. Directed BEQ, JAL and\nJALR tests measured ten-cycle transfer-to-division retirement gaps.\nRequire the full unchanged formal suite and fresh vendor/CRC fitness\nevaluation after implementation. The main practical risk is that the\ncritical path moves to ordinary EX or forwarding logic; the prototype\nalready shows those paths nearly tying the shortened divider.\n'''\nh['expected_impact'] = {'fitness_delta_pct': 50, 'confidence': 'high'}\nh['changes'][0]['description'] = '''Repartition the divider into launch-time registered 32-bit magnitude\npreparation, seven uniform iterations of five restoring bits, and\nDONE result correction consumed on acceptance. Use a 35-bit quotient\nshift register initialized with three leading zeros and the 32-bit\nmagnitude dividend, selecting its low 32 bits for quotient results.\nRemove dedicated PREP/CORRECT cycles, widen the iteration counter,\npreserve exact RV32M exceptions, freeze every result source in DONE,\nand retain exact ALTOPS formulas with identical lifecycle timing.\n'''\nh['changes'][1]['description'] = '''Retain every existing directed and randomized arithmetic assertion.\nUpdate the explicit completion-latency expectation from six to seven\nclocks after launch. Cover reset in every iteration and DONE, ignored\nbusy requests, stable held completion, signed/unsigned exceptional\ncases and subsequent accepted operations in real and ALTOPS builds.\n'''\nh['changes'][2]['description'] = '''Retain the ten-clock retirement-gap ceiling and all existing ordered\nretirement, source refresh, arithmetic and stalled-memory assertions.\nScope the driver's remembered completion sample to the current DONE\ninterval by clearing it when DONE is low; retain the equality check\nthroughout each held completion, allowing later instructions to have\ndifferent results. Extend dependent and independent adjacent division\ncoverage and add taken BEQ/JAL/JALR immediately followed by division,\nchecking PC continuity, exact-once retirement, ten-clock progress and\nsuppression of wrong-path stores with ready and independently stalled\nbuses. Preserve held completion behind older memory operations.\n'''\nclass LiteralDumper(yaml.SafeDumper):\n    pass\ndef represent_string(dumper, value):\n    return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str', value,\n                                   style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nLiteralDumper.add_representer(str, represent_string)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h, Dumper=LiteralDumper, sort_keys=False, width=80))\nvalidate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()), json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert -50 <= h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <= 50\nprint('Refined schema-valid structural hypothesis:', p)\nPY\ntail -n 12 cores/bench/generated/repartition_five_probe/formal_probe/cores/trial/checks/reg_ch0/logfile.txt\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
