gn and exception metadata.\n\nEach of six LOOP edges consumes five of the remaining 30 numerator\nbits. For the radix-8 digit form T={remainder,next_three_bits} as an\nunsigned 35-bit number. Evaluate T-kD for k=1..7 concurrently with\na 36th borrow bit. Select the largest fitting digit 0..7 and its\nmatching residual using monotonic-fit-derived mutually exclusive\nterms and a balanced residual mux. Append that three-bit digit to\nthe quotient shifter. For the radix-4 digit form U={new_remainder,\nnext_two_bits}, evaluate U-D, U-2D and U-3D concurrently with correct\nwidening, and select digit 0..3 and residual the same way. Append the\ntwo-bit digit. Thus each update has two dependent subtract/select\ngroups rather than four or five serial restoring steps. Restored\nremainders fit 32 bits for every nonzero divisor, but trial operands\nand all divisor multiples must retain their full widths. Detect fit\nfrom the extra borrow bit; do not use signed 32-bit comparisons or\ntruncate large multiples. Special divisor-zero state is overridden\nwhen producing the architectural result.\n\nPREP plus six LOOP edges replaces seven LOOP edges, so DONE becomes\nvisible at exactly the existing clock. Keep sign and exceptional\nresult correction on frozen DONE state feeding EX/MEM acceptance,\nwithout adding a completion clock. Ready-bus adjacent DIV/REM\nretirements remain nine clocks apart; unpredicted control transfer\nfollowed by DIV/REM remains within ten clocks. Hold all completed\narithmetic and metadata until accepted, cancel on reset, and keep\nexact-once execution and retirement through arbitrary older dmem\nstalls. Preserve load dependency protection and held operand refresh.\n\nKeep exact DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas with the\nsame launch, PREP, six-LOOP and acceptance lifecycle. Preserve every\nexisting arithmetic test and all nret=1 IO, decoder, RVFI order,\nliveness, cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark correctness gates.\nValidate real arithmetic with signed extrema, zero divisor, overflow,\nhigh divisors, exhaustive seed pairs, all digit boundaries and\nrandomized full operands. Verify the unchanged latency in real and\nALTOPS configurations.\n\nExpected fitness gain is 12 percent with medium confidence. At unchanged\nmarker cycles the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold requires median\nFmax above 57.143 MHz. The main risks are PREP magnitude/multiple delay,\ntrial-mux routing and ordinary ALU or multiply reduction timing.\nRequire unchanged Gowin median timing and CoreMark marker-window\nevaluation. Timing of the proposed two-group engine is still pending;\na three-group radix-4/radix-4/radix-2 scratch engine passed all six\nreal/ALTOPS divider, M-unit and core dependency tests but reached only\n47.911 MHz, motivating the reduction to two dependent groups.\n'''\nh['changes'][0]['description']='''Replace launch-edge magnitude/seed work and seven four-bit restoring\niterations with preserved raw operand capture, one PREP update and\nsix uniform radix-8 then radix-4 quotient updates, seeding two bits\nand consuming five bits per LOOP edge. Register full-width D..7D\nin PREP using parallel shift/add/subtract expressions. Use a 16-entry\ntwo-bit seed lookup, widened parallel trial subtractors and balanced\none-hot residue selection. Preserve exact RV32M sign/exception results,\nfrozen DONE, reset and handshake; retain total latency in real/ALTOPS.\n'''\nh['changes'][1]['description']=h['changes'][1]['description'].replace('four-bit seed','two-bit seed')\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef multiline(dumper, value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nD.add_representer(str,multiline)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=85))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated the two-group divider hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Updated and schema-validated the two-group divider hypothesis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' formal/run_all.sh; rg -n 'RISCV_FORMAL|work_id|WORK_ID|RTL_DIR|CORE_NAME|cp |mktemp' formal/run_all.sh\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_24", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' formal/run_all.sh; rg -n 'RISCV_FORMAL|work_id|WORK_ID|RTL_DIR|CORE_NAME|cp |mktemp' formal/run_all.sh\"", "aggregated_output": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\n#\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\n# logfile.txt.\n#\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\n#\n# Env vars (orchestrator-driven, override $1 and defaults):\n#   WRAPPER     — path to wrapper SV (default formal/wrapper.sv)\n#   CHECKS_CFG  — path to checks config (default formal/checks.cfg)\n# When BOTH are unset, the script auto-detects nret from\n# cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml: nret=1 routes to wrapper_si.sv +\n# checks_si.cfg; nret=2 (or absent) uses the defaults. Agents\n# invoking `bash formal/run_all.sh` directly therefore get the\n# right wrapper without needing to know the env-var plumbing.\nset -e\n\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${CORE_NAME:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: formal/run_all.sh requires RTL_DIR and CORE_NAME env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl CORE_NAME=v1 bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nPROJECT_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nRISCV_FORMAL=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\"\n# Per-invocation work dir + log, unique among every process sharing this\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[5604 of 8676 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:06854766042c8f17]…\n [ -n \"${FORMAL_WORK_ID:-}\" ]; then\n44:    if [[ ! \"$FORMAL_WORK_ID\" =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*$ ]]; then\n45:        echo \"ERROR: FORMAL_WORK_ID must be a letter followed by letters/digits\" >&2\n48:    CORE_DIR=\"$RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-$FORMAL_WORK_ID\"\n51:    # mktemp picks the name; mkdir creates it (atomic, fails if it exists)\n53:    # `mktemp -d` would replace with 0700.\n56:        try=\"$(mktemp -u \"$RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-wXXXXXXXX\")\"\n60:        echo \"ERROR: could not create a work dir under $RISCV_FORMAL/cores\" >&2\n64:WORK_ID=\"${CORE_DIR##*-}\"\n65:LOG=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/last_run-$WORK_ID.log\"\n83:CORE_YAML=\"$PROJECT_ROOT/cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml\"\n114:if [ ! -d \"$RISCV_FORMAL\" ]; then\n134:    for stale in \"$RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-\"*; do\n160:cp \"$PROJECT_ROOT/$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv \"$CORE_DIR/\"\n161:cp \"$WRAPPER\"                   \"$CORE_DIR/wrapper.sv\"\n186:# so cd-ing into $CORE_DIR (= $RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-<suffix>)\n187:# yields @core@=\"$CORE_NAME-<suffix>\". The [verilog-files] lines we just\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"tail -8 /srv/hwebench/tmp/bf842e9a/b4edfa90/radix32_probe/tests.log; sed -n '160,225p' formal/run_all.sh; cat formal/checks_si.cfg; ls -ld formal/riscv-formal; du -sh formal/riscv-formal/checks formal/riscv-formal/insns formal/riscv-formal/tests; ls formal/riscv-formal\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_25", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"tail -8 /srv/hwebench/tmp/bf842e9a/b4edfa90/radix32_probe/tests.log; sed -n '160,225p' formal/run_all.sh; cat formal/checks_si.cfg; ls -ld formal/riscv-formal; du -sh formal/riscv-formal/checks formal/riscv-formal/insns formal/riscv-formal/tests; ls formal/riscv-formal\"", "aggregated_output": "......                                                                   [100%]\n6 passed in 24.61s\ncp \"$PROJECT_ROOT/$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv \"$CORE_DIR/\"\ncp \"$WRAPPER\"                   \"$CORE_DIR/wrapper.sv\"\n\n# Stage checks.cfg with [verilog-files] auto-derived from actual rtl/\n# contents instead of the cfg's hardcoded list. The shipped checks.cfg\n# enumerates the V0 baseline filenames; CLAUDE.md grants hypotheses the\n# right to add new modules, split a stage into multiple files, or rename\n# files in rtl/. Hardcoding silently fails on those restructurings (or\n# elaborates a stale ghost). Strip the original [verilog-files] section\n# and rebuild from the glob, with core_pkg.sv first and wrapper.sv last.\nSTAGED_CFG=\"$CORE_DIR/checks.cfg\"\nawk '/^\\[verilog-files\\]/{exit} {print}' \"$CHECKS_CFG\" > \"$STAGED_CFG\"\n{\n    echo \"[verilog-files]\"\n    [ -f \"$CORE_DIR/core_pkg.sv\" ] && \\\n        echo \"@basedir@/cores/@core@/core_pkg.sv\"\n    for f in \"$CORE_DIR\"/*.sv; do\n        name=\"$(basename \"$f\")\"\n        [ \"$name\" = \"core_pkg.sv\" ] && continue\n        [ \"$name\" = \"wrapper.sv\" ] && continue\n        echo \"@basedir@/cores/@core@/$name\"\n    done\n    echo \"@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\"\n} >> \"$STAGED_CFG\"\n\n# genchecks.py derives @core@ from the cwd's basename (genchecks.py:39),\n# so cd-ing into $CORE_DIR (= $RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-<suffix>)\n# yields @core@=\"$CORE_NAME-<suffix>\". The [verilog-files] lines we just\n# wrote reference that @core@, so all paths resolve into this private dir.\ncd \"$CORE_DIR\"\nrm -rf checks/\necho \"=== genchecks ===\" | tee -a \"$LOG\"\npython3 ../../checks/genchecks.py >> \"$LOG\" 2>&1\ncd checks\n\nJOBS=\"${JOBS:-$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || nproc 2>/dev/null || echo 4)}\"\necho \"=== make -j$JOBS -k ===\" | tee -a \"$LOG\"\n# Tee full output to LOG; show filtered progress on stdout. `-k` keeps\n# going past per-check errors so the .sby tally below sees every result\n# (NRET=2 means a few `_ch1` checks may PREUNSAT-error on single-issue\n# cores; without -k make stops on first error and 95+ checks never run,\n# prod\n…[1779 of 4851 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e88cac9e013100b0]…\nine RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 nobody nogroup 66 Oct  1 05:38 formal/riscv-formal -> /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal\n996K\tformal/riscv-formal/checks\n4.5M\tformal/riscv-formal/insns\n124K\tformal/riscv-formal/tests\nCOPYING\nCodeOfConduct\nREADME.md\nbus\nchecks\ncores\ndocs\ninsns\nmonitor\ntests\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval import gowin\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/bf842e9a/b4edfa90/radix32_probe')\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool:\n results=list(pool.map(lambda o: gowin.build(Path.cwd(),p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',o,p/f'timing_p{o}'),(1,2)))\nfor r in results:\n (p/f\\\"timing_p{r['place_option']}.json\\\").write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\n print(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
