{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261001-003-r5s2':\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\ncommand -v verilator; command -v pytest; ls /opt/gowin/IDE/bin; ls bench/programs\"", "aggregated_output": "Preserve the successful 64x2-bit folded-PC bimodal predictor, but replace\nits reset-all counter implementation with FPGA distributed LUT RAM and\none serialized cold-initialization controller. This is one predictor\nstorage change; retain the current raw legal-branch/JAL predecode,\ndirect-target arithmetic, speculation bounds and alignment checks,\npredicted_taken metadata, direct one-bit EX verification, validity-only\nrecovery and sequential JALR policy.\n\nRemove reset assignment from the counter array. Reset only a seven-bit\ninitialization count to zero. On each non-reset edge while count[6] is\nzero, write 2'b01 to counters[count[5:0]] and increment the count. The\nedge that writes entry 63 changes the count to 64; then hold it at 64\nuntil reset. Predict conditional branches sequentially during these\n64 cold-start clocks, and discard conditional-branch training during\ninitialization because the single RAM write port belongs to the sweep.\nDirect JAL prediction remains active throughout initialization. The\nsweep must run independently of fetch stalls, dmem stalls and M holds;\nit must never stall the CPU or alter architectural next-PC resolution.\nReset restarts the entire sweep, including if it interrupts a sweep.\n\nAfter initialization, use the exact current index PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]\nfor both lookup and training, the same weakly-not-taken state, and\nold-counter simultaneous read/write semantics. Fetch needs the direction\nMSB at fetch_index; training reads the complete old counter at train_index.\nIn