{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='structural':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d['outcome'],d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n  print('CRITICAL:',d.get('critical_path'))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',d.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(d.get('hypothesis'),dict) else d.get('hypothesis'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 Partition DIV and REM across four registered arithmetic steps improvement 68.17 30.617 4491485\nCRITICAL: {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_1_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_10_s0', 'slack_ns': -28.061, 'arrival_ns': 38.355, 'logic_cells': 252, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nHYPOTHESIS: Keep the existing single-issue pipeline and combinational RV32I/MUL\ndatapaths, but execute DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU in a separate registered\ndivider that performs eight radix-2 restoring steps per clock and\ncompletes its 32 quotient bits in four clocks. Remove all / and % operators\nfrom the ordinary ALU. Capture the forwarded operands and instruction\nmetadata once, then use a shared unsigned quotient/remainder engine\nwith at most eight serial 33-bit trial subtract/select updates per\ncycle, rather than the baseline's full 32-step combinational divide.\nUse separate registered magnitude preparation and final sign\ncorrection; no cycle combines those with the quotient loop. Return a\nresult within six clocks after accepting operands: one magnitude\npreparation clock, four quotient clocks and one correction clock.\nHandle divide-by-zero and signed overflow explicitly. MUL, MULH,\nMULHU and MULHSU remain unchanged. This is one divider-topology change;\nretain the rest of the pipeline and avoid unrelated throughput work.\n\nA valid division occupies EX until its result is available. Freeze\nfetch/decode and prevent younger instructions from entering EX; older\ninstructions continue through MEM/WB. Once the previous EX/MEM entry\nis accepted by MEM, replace it with an invalid entry while the divider\nruns rather than repeatedly retiring it. A stalled older data-memory\nrequest keeps its EX/MEM payload and bus outputs stable. Latch the\ndivider's result and all RVFI metadata until EX/MEM can accept them,\nthen enqueue the instruction exactly once. Capture operands only on\na single accepted start event, using existing forwarding, and preserve\nheld ID/EX source values when their producers drain from MEM/WB.\nRegis\n…[30305 of 33377 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8105db992bde1f52]…\nvely, which is\nwhy neither isolated edit is the proposed implementation. This is\nprototype evidence; require fresh evaluator results for acceptance.\nPlacement options 0/1/2 measured 69.617/73.220/73.220 MHz, giving\nmedian 73.220 MHz. With the verified unchanged marker window this\ncomputes to 167.239 iterations/second, 14.063 percent above 146.62\nand beyond the 4.6 percent acceptance margin.\n\nExpected fitness gain is 14 percent with high confidence from this\ncomplete prototype validation. At the unchanged cycle count\nacceptance requires median Fmax approximately above 67.15\nMHz and fitness above 153.36452 iterations/second. Evaluate all three\nvendor placement options. The main risks are flop/read-tree area and\nrouting, incorrect held-selector transitions, synthesis crossing\nthe intended boundary, and scalar arithmetic or the divider becoming\nthe next timing limit. Retain every correctness gate and test\nexpectation; adjust internal hierarchy helpers only as necessary\nwithout relaxing architectural assertions.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
