{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(e['id'], e.get('outcome'),e.get('fitness'),e.get('fmax_mhz'),e.get('cycles'),e.get('title'))\n  h=e.get('hypothesis', {})\n  print('MOTIVATION:', h.get('motivation') if isinstance(h,dict) else e.get('motivation'))\n  if e['id'].endswith(('r6s2','r8s2','r9s2')):\n   print('PROPOSAL:',h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  notes=e.get('implementation_notes')\n  if notes: print('NOTES:',notes[:7000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 regression 13.51 5.66 4190307 64-entry folded-PC bimodal prediction at fetch\nMOTIVATION: The baseline has no branch prediction: every aligned taken conditional\nbranch and every JAL/JALR redirects in EX and discards the current fetch.\nIF/ID is combinational, so a correctly predicted direct transfer can\neliminate the one-cycle recovery bubble without adding a pipeline stage.\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loops and direct calls, making a small\nadaptive direction predictor a promising way to increase iterations/cycle.\nProfiling the canonical 2K, -O3, 10-iteration instruction stream between\nits MMIO markers finds 529588 conditional branches, of which 294425 are\ntaken, plus 49367 direct JALs. A 64-entry table with a folded PC index\npredicts about 92.2 percent of conditional branches correctly.\nThe measured critical path is the forwarded combinational M-extension\nALU, not branch lookup; this proposal targets throughput while keeping\npredictor storage and added fetch logic small enough to preserve Fmax.\n\nhyp-20261001-003-r2s2 regression 75.79 31.759 4190281 Compact bimodal direct-transfer prediction on the registered-divider champion\nMOTIVATION: The current champion has the registered DIV/REM unit but still fetches\nsequentially and loses one fetch cycle on every taken branch and jump.\nLESSONS.md and the hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 experiment show that a 64-entry\nfolded-PC bimodal predictor plus direct JAL prediction reduced canonical\nCoreMark's bracketed cycles from 4491485 to 4190307, a 7.19 percent\nthroughput gain. That attempt used the old combinational divider and\ntherefore achieved only 5.66 MHz. The champion achieves 30.617 MHz with\nthe same 4491485-cycle window, so its absence of prediction leaves this\nmeasured throughput opportunity available. This proposal applies the\npredictor to the current champion and keeps the registered divider intact.\n\nhyp-20261001-003-r3s2 improvement 112.68 47.218 4190281 Compact bimodal prediction with validity-only recovery\nMOTIVATION: The five-step registered-divider c\n…[18378 of 21450 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5e44b99fd8b82259]…\nclock progress bounds, formal/cosim gates,\ncanonical CRC/UART/bounds/markers and exactly 4378154 CoreMark cycles.\n\nAn isolated compact three-plane prototype using COMBINATIONAL plane\nmasks measured 72.427/77.878/77.878 MHz across Gowin options 0/1/2:\nmedian 77.878 MHz, 6599 logic cells and 2170 registers. That is only a\n1.85 percent frequency gain and does not pass acceptance. This proposal\nspecifically adds the decode-registered one-hot mask to remove its\nremaining encoded-selector decode and plane-arbitration control cone.\nThat final refinement has not been timed; confidence is low. A broader\nnine-source-pair prototype passed all 31 current pipeline tests but\nregressed timing, supporting the compact scope rather than replication\nacross every source pair. Require fresh correctness gates and all three\nvendor placements for the final implementation. At unchanged cycles,\nacceptance needs median Fmax above 79.983436 MHz and fitness above\n182.6839 iterations/second; ALU or divider recurrence can still limit it.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
