2594, \"oob\": false}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"agent_message","text":"The simpler update paths also missed the timing target. A fresh CoreMark trace shows a more promising predictor angle: 135,320 conditional branches immediately follow a load they consume, and the existing predictor misses only 2,190 of them. I’ll revise the hypothesis to let those branches follow their prediction and verify the loaded operand in MEM, reducing load-use bubbles."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_29", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r10s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-003-r10s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict through load-dependent branches and verify them in MEM\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.65 at 76.466 MHz and 4378154 CoreMark marker\n  cycles. Prior attempts established that direction accuracy alone has\n  only about 1.3 percent remaining cycle benefit. Carried successors,\n  local counter updates, alternate correction rails and comparator planes\n  have not displaced the champion. An isolated training-register prototype\n  in this investigation preserved the marker cycles and canonical CRCs\n  but reached only 73.345 MHz median, with divider recurrence limiting it.\n  The proposal therefore targets a different source of predictor value:\n  using a correct direction prediction while a branch operand is pending.\n\n  A fresh run of the unmodified reference ISS on the canonical 2K/-O3/\n  ten-iteration ELF counted 2922016 instructions and 529588 conditional\n  branches between the MMIO markers. Of those branches, 135320 immediately\n  follow a load whose destination is one of their sources. The existing\n  64-entry folded-PC bimodal model misses only 2190 of these branches,\n  approximately 1.62 percent. The current hazard unit nevertheless inserts\n  a load-use bubble whenever such a branch is fetched while imem is ready.\n  These highly predictable branches provide a throughput opportunity that\n  is absent from the previous direction-error-only bound.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the existing direction predictor to issue an aligned legal conditional\n  branch behind its immediately preceding load without the load-use bubble,\n  and defer ONLY that branch's operand verification to the existing MEM\n  stage. The branch follows its already accepted predicted successor at\n  fetch. It remains an in-order pipeline token and retires at the existing\n  MEM/WB boundary after its real operands and next PC have been resolved.\n  This is one predictor-assisted verification change, with no global stage\n  addition and no changes to the BHT's capacity, index, counter transitions,\n  reset state, direct JAL prediction or speculative address bounds.\n\n  Exempt a load-use dependency only when the fetched instruction is a\n  decoder-validated conditional branch with an aligned direct target and\n  the valid immediately older EX token is a load to a nonzero matching\n  source register. Require actual fetch acceptance; absent imem data still\n  produces the existing bubble. Capture two pending-source bits and the\n  expected load destination with the ID/EX branch payload. Preserve the\n  normal load-use interlock for every other instruction, reserved branch\n  encoding, misaligned direct branch, JALR and M operation. Keep older\n  data-memory stalls authoritative: they hold the load in MEM and branch\n  in EX until the load is accepted, without issuing or verifying the branch\n  twice. Do not add a combinational dmem-data-to-EX comparison bypass.\n\n  A marked branch traverses EX without resolving, redirecting or training\n  there. EX captures its ordinary forwarded fallback operands, accepted\n  prediction, full direct and sequential successors, registered recovery\n  predicates, source metadata and pending-source bits into EX/MEM. The\n  fallback is important if the older load traps and does not write a\n  register: maintain the previous architectural source value using the\n  existing RF/forwarding/held-WB refresh discipline. Never substitute an\n  effective address or invalid load value for the branch operand.\n\n  In MEM, replace a marked source with the actual immediately older load's\n  WB value only when that WB token is valid, writes the captured destination\n  and is non-trapping; otherwise retain the captured fallback. Resolve all\n  six RV32I branch conditions with these operands and the carried decode\n  recovery predicates. Set the branch's MEM/WB RVFI source values and\n  pc_next to the resolved architectural result, preserving its normal\n  retirement order and trap policy. Train the BHT from this actual outcome\n  exactly once. A correct prediction permits the younger EX instruction to\n  advance normally, saving the load-use bubble without a new retirement\n  stage. Non-marked branches retain their current EX verification schedule.\n\n  A MEM mismatch is an older recovery than anything currently in EX. It\n  redirects fetch immediately to the full architectural direct/fallthrough\n  successor, invalidates fetch and ID/EX, and cancels the younger EX enqueue\n  on that edge. Clear the younger EX/MEM valid bit and memory masks before\n  that instruction can drive a memory request. Suppress its EX redirect,\n  training, producer eligibility and M-unit launch as well as architectural\n  writes. An older recovery must override frontend holds and any apparent\n  younger load-use/M hold; do not let ex_hold prevent the squash. A younger\n  MUL/DIV must not start on this recovery edge. Ordinary EX recovery remains\n  unchanged when there is no MEM mismatch. Qualify both recovery sources\n  by their actual token validity and acceptance, never by raw retained\n  payload. Recovery must continue to override an instruction-bus stall.\n\n  Integrate correction candidates as mutually exclusive MEM and EX rails\n  into the existing single fetch-PC register so adding older recovery does\n  not insert a serial target-priority mux into every current EX-to-fetch\n  path. Carry stable successor values before comparisons; qualify the EX\n  rail with absence of an older MEM correction. Combine correction and\n  normal predicted-advance/hold candidates with balanced masked OR logic.\n  Retain immediate validity-only squash and full 32-bit correction targets.\n  This integration is part of the deferred-verification mechanism; avoid\n  unrelated RF, ALU, divider, cache or instruction-queue changes.\n\n  MEM verification and a younger ordinary EX branch can generate two\n  training events on one edge. Preserve chronological training with the\n  existing single-write BHT and a one-entry event skid register: train the\n  older MEM event first and retain the younger EX event. When an event is\n  pending, consume it before current EX training and retain a current event\n  if needed. A deferred branch emits no EX event on the preceding edge,\n  which provides a drain slot before its MEM event, so this specific\n  immediate-load deferral pattern needs only one queued event. Assert this\n  occupancy property and check all simultaneous cases; never overwrite a\n  pending event or replay it during stalls. Reset clears deferred validity\n  and pending training. Lookup observes the old table on the update edge;\n  the exact accepted predicted_taken bit remains the recovery reference.\n\n  The instruction trace gives an upper bound of approximately 133130 saved\n  clocks before random bus overlap: one removed bubble per eligible branch\n  less roughly one extra recovery clock per prediction error. About 22\n  percent imem stalls already hide some baseline load-use bubbles, so use\n  approximately 100000 saved marker clocks, around 2.3 percent, as the\n  conservative cycle target rather than claiming the full trace bound.\n  At 4278154 cycles the 4.6 percent acceptance bar requires median Fmax\n  above approximately 78.16 MHz. Removing the generic EX operand mux from\n  verification of these load-dependent branches makes a modest timing\n  gain plausible, but the unchanged divider and added recovery arbitration\n  are substantial risks. Expect approximately five percent fitness gain\n  with low confidence; measure actual cycles and all vendor placements.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, all external ports, decoder default-illegal behavior,\n  conditional alignment traps, canonical CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks,\n  ordered exact-once retirement, real and ALTOPS M behavior and every\n  unmodified formal gate. Verify both pending sources, load faults, x0,\n  youngest-writer forwarding, branch taken/not-taken recovery, reserved and\n  misaligned encodings, simultaneous EX/MEM corrections, wrong-path stores\n  and M launches, arbitrary imem/dmem stalls and reset cancellation. Do not\n  relax liveness or architectural assertions to accommodate this mechanism.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Carry deferred conditional-branch source markers, expected older load destination, accepted prediction, full successors and recovery predicates across the existing ID/EX and EX/MEM boundaries, preserving architectural payloads.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Exempt only accepted legal aligned conditional branches with an immediate older-load source dependency from the load-use bubble. Preserve all other interlocks and make older MEM recovery cancel younger EX work and dominate frontend/M holds.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Capture the selective deferred-verification markers with the branch payload using validated decode and aligned direct target; retain ordinary forwarding selection, held operand refresh and validity-only squash.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Pass marked branches to MEM without premature resolution or training, preserving forwarded fallback operands and prediction metadata. Add older-recovery cancellation of EX enqueue, request masks, producer eligibility, redirects, training and M launch. Keep ordinary EX execution and arithmetic unchanged.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Verify marked branches using qualified immediately older WB load data, update their RVFI operands and resolved next PC at the existing MEM/WB edge, and emit oldest-priority same-cycle correction and exactly-once predictor training. Retain fallback operands when the older load does not write, and preserve all memory request/retirement behavior.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Accept mutually exclusive MEM and EX correction rails into the existing PC register while preserving bounded prediction and recovery-over-stall priority. Serialize ordered simultaneous training events with one skid entry and assert that it cannot overflow under immediate-load branch deferral.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire qualified WB load data, selective load-branch deferral, older MEM correction, EX kill and training arbitration without changing external ports or the nret=1 contract.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Extend the independent predictor/pipeline model for deferred branches and ordered training, preserving all architectural assertions. Add directed source dependencies, qualified versus faulted loads, both recovery directions, simultaneous younger EX control flow, squashed stores/M launches, stalls and reset; distinguish eligible branch bubble removal from retained ordinary load-use stalls.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: \\\"Check correction priority, old-counter lookup and chronological two-event training serialization, pending-event drain during stalls, skid occupancy and reset cancellation while retaining predecode, alignment, bounds and counter-transition coverage.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_core_div.py\n    description: \\\"Extend mixed branch/M tests to cover an older deferred-branch mismatch coinciding with a younger multiply/divide launch, requiring no wrong-path launch or retirement and preserving real/ALTOPS arithmetic and existing ready-bus progress bounds.\\\"\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nprint('Required Fmax at 4278154 cycles:',174.65*1.046*4278154/10/1e6)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_29", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r10s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-003-r10s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict through load-dependent branches and verify them in MEM\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.65 at 76.466 MHz and 4378154 CoreMark marker\n  cycles. Prior attempts established that direction accuracy alone has\n  only about 1.3 percent remaining cycle benefit. Carried successors,\n  local counter updates, alternate correction rails and comparator planes\n  have not displaced the champion. An isolated training-register prototype\n  in this investigation preserved the marker cycles and canonical CRCs\n  but reached only 73.345 MHz median, with divider recurrence limiting it.\n  The proposal therefore targets a different source of predictor value:\n  using a correct direction prediction while a branch operand is pending.\n\n  A fresh run of the unmodified reference ISS on the canonical 2K/-O3/\n  ten-iteration ELF counted 2922016 instructions and 529588 conditional\n  branches between the MMIO markers. Of those branches, 135320 immediately\n  follow a load whose destination is one of their sources. The existing\n  64-entry folded-PC bimodal model misses only 2190 of these branches,\n  approximately 1.62 percent. The current hazard unit nevertheless inserts\n  a load-use bubble whenever such a branch is fetched while imem is ready.\n  These highly predictable branches provide a throughput opportunity that\n  is absent from the previous direction-error-only bound.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the existing direction predictor to issue an aligned legal conditional\n  branch behind its immediately preceding load without the load-use bubble,\n  and defer ONLY that branch's operand verification to the existing MEM\n  stage. The branch follows its already accepted predicted successor at\n  fetch. It remains an in-order pipeline token and retires at the existing\n  MEM/WB boundary after its real operands and next PC have been resolved.\n  This is one predictor-assisted verification change, with no global stage\n  addition and no changes to the BHT's capacity, index, counter transitions,\n  reset state, direct JAL prediction or speculative address bounds.\n\n  Exempt a load-use dependency only when the fetched instruction is a\n  decoder-validated conditional branch with an aligned direct target and\n  the valid immediately older EX token is a load to a nonzero matching\n  source register. Require actual fetch acceptance; absent imem data still\n  produces the existing bubble. Capture two pending-source bits and the\n  expected load destination with the ID/EX branch payload. Preserve the\n  normal load-use interlock for every other instruction, reserved branch\n  encoding, misaligned direct branch, JALR and M operation. Keep older\n  data-memory stalls authoritative: they hold the load in MEM and branch\n  in EX until the load is accepted, without issuing or verifying the branch\n  twice. Do not add a combinational dmem-data-to-EX comparison bypass.\n\n  A marked branch traverses EX without resolving, redirecting or training\n  there. EX captures its ordinary forwarded fallback operands, accepted\n  prediction, full direct and sequential successors, registered recovery\n  predicates, source metadata and pending-source bits into EX/MEM. The\n  fallback is important if the older load traps and does not write a\n  register: maintain the previous architectural source value using the\n  existing RF/forwarding/held-WB refresh discipline. Never substitute an\n  effective address or invalid load value for the branch operand.\n\n  In MEM, replace a marked source with the actual immediately older load's\n  WB value only when that WB token is valid, writes the captured destination\n  and is non-trapping; otherwise retain the captured fallback. Resolve all\n  six RV32I branch conditions with these operands and the carried decode\n  recovery predicates. Set the branch's MEM/WB RVFI source values and\n  pc_next to the resolved architectural result, preserving its normal\n  retirement order and trap policy. Train the BHT from this actual outcome\n  exactly once. A correct prediction permits the younger EX instruction to\n  advance normally, saving the load-use bubble without a new retirement\n  stage. Non-marked branches retain their current EX verification schedule.\n\n  A MEM mismatch is an older recovery than anything currently in EX. It\n  redirects fetch immediately to the full architectural direct/fallthrough\n  successor, invalidates fetch and ID/EX, and cancels the younger EX enqueue\n  on that edge. Clear the younger EX/MEM valid bit and memory masks before\n  that instruction can drive a memory request. Suppress its EX redirect,\n  training, producer eligibility and M-unit launch as well as architectural\n  writes. An older recovery must override frontend holds and any apparent\n  younger load-use/M hold; do not let ex_hold prevent the squash. A younger\n  MUL/DIV must not start on this recovery edge. Ordinary EX recovery remains\n  unchanged when there is no MEM mismatch. Qualify both recovery sources\n  by their actual token validity and acceptance, never by raw retained\n  payload. Recovery must continue to override an instruction-bus stall.\n\n  Integrate correction candidates as mutually exclusive MEM and EX rails\n  into the existing single fetch-PC register so adding older recovery does\n  not insert a serial target-priority mux into every current EX-to-fetch\n  path. Carry stable successor values before comparisons; qualify the EX\n  rail with absence of an older MEM correction. Combine correction and\n  normal predicted-advance/hold candidates with balanced masked OR logic.\n  Retain immediate validity-only squash and full 32-bit correction targets.\n  This integration is part of the deferred-verification mechanism; avoid\n  unrelated RF, ALU, divider, cache or instruction-queue changes.\n\n  MEM verification and a younger ordinary EX branch can generate two\n  training events on one edge. Preserve chronological training with the\n  existing single-write BHT and a one-entry event skid register: train the\n  older MEM event first and retain the younger EX event. When an event is\n  pending, consume it before current EX training and retain a current event\n  if needed. A deferred branch emits no EX event on the preceding edge,\n  which provides a drain slot before its MEM event, so this specific\n  immediate-load deferral pattern needs only one queued event. Assert this\n  occupancy property and check all simultaneous cases; never overwrite a\n  pending event or replay it during stalls. Reset clears deferred validity\n  and pending training. Lookup observes the old table on the update edge;\n  the exact accepted predicted_taken bit remains the recovery reference.\n\n  The instruction trace gives an upper bound of approximately 133130 saved\n  clocks before random bus overlap: one removed bubble per eligible branch\n  less roughly one extra recovery clock per prediction error. About 22\n  percent imem stalls already hide some baseline load-use bubbles, so use\n  approximately 100000 saved marker clocks, around 2.3 percent, as the\n  conservative cycle target rather than claiming the full trace bound.\n  At 4278154 cycles the 4.6 percent acceptance bar requires median Fmax\n  above approximately 78.16 MHz. Removing the generic EX operand mux from\n  verification of these load-dependent branches makes a modest timing\n  gain plausible, but the unchanged divider and added recovery arbitration\n  are substantial risks. Expect approximately five percent fitness gain\n  with low confidence; measure actual cycles and all vendor placements.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, all external ports, decoder default-illegal behavior,\n  conditional alignment traps, canonical CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks,\n  ordered exact-once retirement, real and ALTOPS M behavior and every\n  unmodified formal gate. Verify both pending sources, load faults, x0,\n  youngest-writer forwarding, branch taken/not-taken recovery, reserved and\n  misaligned encodings, simultaneous EX/MEM corrections, wrong-path stores\n  and M launches, arbitrary imem/dmem stalls and reset cancellation. Do not\n  relax liveness or architectural assertions to accommodate this mechanism.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Carry deferred conditional-branch source markers, expected older load destination, accepted prediction, full successors and recovery predicates across the existing ID/EX and EX/MEM boundaries, preserving architectural payloads.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Exempt only accepted legal aligned conditional branches with an immediate older-load source dependency from the load-use bubble. Preserve all other interlocks and make older MEM recovery cancel younger EX work and dominate frontend/M holds.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Capture the selective deferred-verification markers with the branch payload using validated decode and aligned direct target; retain ordinary forwarding selection, held operand refresh and validity-only squash.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Pass marked branches to MEM without premature resolution or training, preserving forwarded fallback operands and prediction metadata. Add older-recovery cancellation of EX enqueue, request masks, producer eligibility, redirects, training and M launch. Keep ordinary EX execution and arithmetic unchanged.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Verify marked branches using qualified immediately older WB load data, update their RVFI operands and resolved next PC at the existing MEM/WB edge, and emit oldest-priority same-cycle correction and exactly-once predictor training. Retain fallback operands when the older load does not write, and preserve all memory request/retirement behavior.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Accept mutually exclusive MEM and EX correction rails into the existing PC register while preserving bounded prediction and recovery-over-stall priority. Serialize ordered simultaneous training events with one skid entry and assert that it cannot overflow under immediate-load branch deferral.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire qualified WB load data, selective load-branch deferral, older MEM correction, EX kill and training arbitration without changing external ports or the nret=1 contract.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Extend the independent predictor/pipeline model for deferred branches and ordered training, preserving all architectural assertions. Add directed source dependencies, qualified versus faulted loads, both recovery directions, simultaneous younger EX control flow, squashed stores/M launches, stalls and reset; distinguish eligible branch bubble removal from retained ordinary load-use stalls.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: \\\"Check correction priority, old-counter lookup and chronological two-event training serialization, pending-event drain during stalls, skid occupancy and reset cancellation while retaining predecode, alignment, bounds and counter-transition coverage.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_core_div.py\n    description: \\\"Extend mixed branch/M tests to cover an older deferred-branch mismatch coinciding with a younger multiply/divide launch, requiring no wrong-path launch or retirement and preserving real/ALTOPS arithmetic and existing ready-bus progress bounds.\\\"\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nprint('Required