{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor' and any(x in d['id'] for x in ['-r4s2','-r5s2','-r7s2']):\n  h=d['hypothesis']; print(d['id']); print(h['motivation']); print(h['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r4s2\nThe champion already has a 64-entry folded-PC bimodal predictor and\ndirect JAL prediction. Earlier modeling found negligible benefit from\nincreasing its direction-table capacity, so another direction-only\nchange is unlikely to clear the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold.\nFetch already computes the direct target and sequential successor,\nbut EX recomputes PC-relative targets and PC+4 while verifying the\naccepted prediction. Reusing registered successors can reduce target\narithmetic, mux depth and routing in the predictor correction path\nwithout adding a pipeline stage or a recovery cycle.\n\nChange the direct-control-flow predictor to carry the successor pair\nit computes at fetch into ID/EX: a full-width sequential PC and a\nfull-width raw B/J direct target. Preserve the existing 64x2-bit\nfolded-PC direction table, reset state, old-counter lookup semantics,\nlegal raw predecode, aligned in-range speculative-target gate and\ndirect JAL prediction. Keep JALR predicted sequentially.\n\nCompute direct_target and sequential_pc from raw imem_data and the\nregistered fetch PC. Capture both with the same payload enable as\nPC, instruction and predicted_taken. Hold them on load-use, divider\nand data-memory holds. Recovery still kills validity alone; it must\nnever gate the wide payload capture or held WB operand refresh.\nCarry the raw target even when it is misaligned or outside instruction\nmemory: the bounds check suppresses speculation only and must not\ntruncate or alter architectural target resolution.\n\nIn EX, reuse the captured direct target for legal conditional branches\nand JAL, and the sequential successor for fallthrough, jump link data,\ntrap fallback and divider metadata. Keep the forwarded rs1+immediate\nJALR calculation and its bit-zero clearing unchanged. Use the raw\ncaptured direct target for branch/JAL misalignment checks. Preserve\nactual_transfer XOR predicted_taken recovery, once-per-advance legal\nconditional training and architectural RVFI pc_wdata. Do not carry a\nredundant predicted-next-PC comp\n…[9511 of 12583 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4326c27507f851c5]…\n 4378154 CoreMark marker cycles before accepting timing evidence.\nAt unchanged cycles the 4.6 percent threshold requires median Fmax\nabove 67.143786 MHz and fitness above 153.36452. An eight percent\nfrequency gain would yield about 158.35 iterations/second. Placement\nmay instead move the bottleneck to arithmetic or the address-feedback\nmux, so the gain remains unmeasured until the vendor flow runs.\n\nPreserve nret=1, every external port, all decoder/trap rules and all\nexisting tests and eval gates. Validate cycle-exact address equivalence\nagainst the original fetch-control model, especially corrections\ncoincident with imem stalls and load-use holds, a stalled first target\nfetch, back-to-back transfers, wrong-path memory/M instructions and\nreset while the registered selector is set. Retain the existing\nbranch-to-DIV/REM ten-clock assertions. Require cosim, formal progress,\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers and three-placement median\nGowin timing; inspect that the new boundaries remain in the netlist.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
