{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(l)\n if x['id']=='hyp-20261001-002-r11s1':\n  print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replace IF's single selected PC register with two preserved 32-bit\ncandidate registers and a one-bit recovery_pending register. One\ncandidate is the ordinary predicted-next-PC cursor; the other captures\nthe existing EX redirect_target independently of the late redirect\ncondition. The effective fetch address is recovery_pending ?\nrecovery_target_q : cursor_q. Use this effective address everywhere\nthe old PC was consumed: io_imemAddr, the fetched PC payload, folded\nBHT lookup, direct-target calculation and sequential successor.\nPair this split PC state with a one-cycle registered BHT training event:\nvalid, taken and the existing six-bit folded training index. This is\none structural retiming of execute-to-fetch feedback, without an\nadditional instruction or recovery stage, queue, predictor capacity/\nindexing change, arithmetic change or delayed branch verification.\n\nLet advance = !stall using the existing qualified frontend stall.\nAfter reset, cursor_q takes the predicted successor of the EFFECTIVE\naddress whenever advance is true and otherwise holds. It need not\nprioritize the current EX redirect: a redirect on that edge selects\nthe separately captured recovery candidate after the edge. Set\nrecovery_pending on the existing qualified redirect regardless of\nfrontend stalls; otherwise clear it on advance and retain it on a\nstall. Reset clears pending and restores the ordinary reset cursor.\nCapture recovery_target_q whenever !recovery_pending || advance,\nholding it while a pending corrected fetch is blocked. Crucially,\nthis wide register's capture enable does not depend on the current\noperand comparison or redirect. When redirect sets