{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json; git status --short'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +462.46%): Replacing ALU `/` and `%` with four registered groups of eight restoring steps and separate magnitude/sign stages shortens the critical path enough to outweigh six-cycle DIV/REM latency in CoreMark's division-light timed kernels.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 (regression, +11.47%): A 64-entry folded-PC BHT plus direct JAL prediction raised CoreMark throughput 7.19% by avoiding EX flush bubbles, but the forwarded combinational DIV/REM path still limited Fmax to 5.66 MHz, making divider pipelining the higher-impact change.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r2s1 (improvement, +54.58%): Reducing div_unit's serial restoring steps per register from eight to five improves timing without increasing CoreMark's marker cycles, while folding magnitude/sign work into launch/accept edges preserves the ten-clock branch-to-DIV liveness bound despite seven LOOP clocks.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +11.18%): A 64-entry BHT/JAL predictor with one-bit direction recovery removes CoreMark flush bubbles while retaining divider-limited timing, but eight serial restoring steps per divider clock leave Fmax at 31.76 MHz versus the five-step divider's 47.33 MHz.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 (improvement, +6.93%): Pairing a 64-entry BHT/JAL predictor with validity-only ID/EX recovery removes correct-prediction flush bubbles without routing EX redirects through instruction masking or wide payload enables, making prediction profitable on the five-step divider.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r4s1 (improvement, +10.74%): With MUL and DIV tied for the critical path, registering MUL limb products and carry-save reduction while seeding four DIV bits at launch shortens both arithmetic cones enough to offset CoreMark's two extra MUL clocks without lengthening the seven-loop divider schedule.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r5s1 (improvement, +8.11%): Consuming five quotient bits per LOOP with parallel radix-8/radix-4 trials frees a PREP clock for preserved raw-operand \n…[4489 of 7561 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:239c1cb4a0aaa5bd]…\nnLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/test/test_if_predictor.py; rg --files cores/bench/experiments; rg --files bench test tools/eval | head -70'", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"Physical prediction snapshots, legality and one-edge delayed training.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb_tools.runner import get_runner\nfrom _helpers import RTL, SIM_BUILD, TEST_DIR\nfrom test_pipeline import BEQ, JAL, JALR, NOP, _b\nfrom test_fetch_queue import reset_fetch, fetch_tick, seek_pc\n\n\nasync def probe(dut, ref, pc, instr):\n    await seek_pc(dut, ref, pc)\n    token = await fetch_tick(dut, ref, instr)\n    return bool(token[2])\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def counter_reset_saturation_alias_and_old_lookup(dut):\n    ref = await reset_fetch(dut)\n    for index in range(64):\n        assert not await probe(dut, ref, index * 4, BEQ(0, 0, 8))\n    await seek_pc(dut, ref, 0x100)\n    # Capture without updating the counter or advancing physical PC.\n    await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False, training=(4, 1))\n    assert ref.counters[1] == 1\n    # The update edge's alias lookup still sees 01, without a bypass.\n    token = await fetch_tick(dut, ref, BEQ(0, 0, 8), hold=True)\n    assert token == (0x100, BEQ(0, 0, 8), 0) and ref.counters[1] == 2\n    await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False, hold=True, training=(4, 0))\n    await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False, hold=True)\n    assert ref.counters[1] == 1 and ref.fifo[0][2] == 0\n    await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False)\n    for outcome in (1, 0, 1):\n        await seek_pc(dut, ref, 4)\n        before = ref.counters[1]\n        for event in range(6):\n            await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False, hold=True,\n                             training=(4 if event % 2 else 0x100, outcome))\n            expected = min(3, before + event) if outcome else max(0, before - event)\n            assert ref.counters[1] == expected\n        await fetch_tick(dut, ref, ready=False, hold=True)\n        assert ref.counters[1] == (3 if outcome else 0)\n        assert await probe(dut, ref, 4, BEQ(0, 0, 8)) == bool(outcome)\n    ref = await reset_fetch(dut)\n    assert not await probe(dut, ref, 4, BEQ(0, 0, 8))\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def legal_predecode_alignment_and_bounds(dut):\n…[4569 of 7641 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0254f244a171f6a3]…\nch/programs/coremark/coremark.h\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.md5\nbench/programs/coremark/core_list_join.c\nbench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c\nbench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.h\nbench/programs/coremark/posix/core_portme.mak\nbench/programs/coremark/posix/core_portme_posix_overrides.h\nbench/programs/coremark/posix/core_portme.c\nbench/programs/coremark/posix/core_portme.h\nbench/programs/coremark/core_state.c\nbench/programs/coremark/simple/core_portme.mak\nbench/programs/coremark/simple/core_portme.c\nbench/programs/coremark/simple/core_portme.h\nbench/programs/coremark/barebones_porting.md\nbench/programs/coremark/linux/core_portme.mak\nbench/programs/coremark/Makefile\nbench/programs/coremark/core_matrix.c\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/coremark_profile_o0_joined.png\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/READM.md\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/balance_O0_joined.png\nbench/programs/coremark/freebsd/core_portme.mak\nbench/programs/coremark/macos/core_portme.mak\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/html/index.html\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
