{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n e=json.loads(l)\n print(e['id'], e['category'], e.get('fitness'), e.get('cycles'), e.get('fmax_mhz'), e.get('outcome'))\n if e['category']=='predictor':\n  h=e['hypothesis']\n  print('Motivation:',h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('Notes:', e.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-4a6c0e6 micro_opt 12.12 4491485 5.442 improvement\nhyp-20261001-001-r1s0 micro_opt 13.98 4491485 6.277 regression\nhyp-20261001-002-r1s1 structural 68.17 4491485 30.617 improvement\nhyp-20261001-003-r1s2 predictor 13.51 4190307 5.66 regression\nMotivation: The baseline has no branch prediction: every aligned taken conditional\nbranch and every JAL/JALR redirects in EX and discards the current fetch.\nIF/ID is combinational, so a correctly predicted direct transfer can\neliminate the one-cycle recovery bubble without adding a pipeline stage.\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loops and direct calls, making a small\nadaptive direction predictor a promising way to increase iterations/cycle.\nProfiling the canonical 2K, -O3, 10-iteration instruction stream between\nits MMIO markers finds 529588 conditional branches, of which 294425 are\ntaken, plus 49367 direct JALs. A 64-entry table with a folded PC index\npredicts about 92.2 percent of conditional branches correctly.\nThe measured critical path is the forwarded combinational M-extension\nALU, not branch lookup; this proposal targets throughput while keeping\npredictor storage and added fetch logic small enough to preserve Fmax.\n\nNotes: \nhyp-20261001-001-r2s0 micro_opt 88.33 4491485 39.675 regression\nhyp-20261001-002-r2s1 structural 105.38 4491485 47.33 improvement\nhyp-20261001-003-r2s2 predictor 75.79 4190281 31.759 regression\nMotivation: The current champion has the registered DIV/REM unit but still fetches\nsequentially and loses one fetch cycle on every taken branch and jump.\nLESSONS.md and the hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 experiment show that a 64-entry\nfolded-PC bimodal predictor plus direct JAL prediction reduced canonical\nCoreMark's bracketed cycles from 4491485 to 4190307, a 7.19 percent\nthroughput gain. That attempt used the old combinational divider and\ntherefore achieved only 5.66 MHz. The champion achieves 30.617 MHz with\nthe same 4491485-cycle window, so its absence of prediction leaves this\nmeasured throughput opportunity available. This proposal applies the\npredictor to the current champion and keeps the registered divider intact.\n\nNotes: \nhyp-20261001-001-r3s0 micro_opt 103.47 4491485 46.475 regression\nhyp-20261001-002-r3s1 structural 103.85 4491485 46.646 regression\nhyp-20261001-003-r3s2 predictor 112.68 4190281 47.218 improvement\nMotivation: The five-step registered-divider champion reaches 47.33 MHz and 105.38\niterations/second, but still spends 4491485 CoreMark marker cycles\nfetching sequentially. Previous predictor attempts demonstrated that\na 64-entry folded-PC bimodal table plus direct JAL prediction reduces\nthis window to approximately 4190281 cycles, a 7.19 percent throughput\ngain. Those attempts used earlier, slower dividers; their regression\nclassification does not establish that prediction is unprofitable on\nthe current champion. Its worst reported path now crosses u_id, u_ex,\nu_if, u_hazard and returns to an ID/EX operand register (27.119 ns).\nAdding prediction must therefore address recovery fanout as part of\nthe predictor integration, rather than put another wide compare and\ninstruction-masking mux on that path.\n\nNotes: \nhyp-20261001-001-r4s0 micro_opt 120.35 4190281 50.432 regression\nhyp-20261001-002-r4s1 structural 124.78 4378154 54.63 improvement\nhyp-20261001-003-r4s2 predictor 112.78 4190281 47.259 regression\nMotivation: The champion already has a 64-entry folded-PC bimodal predictor and\ndirect JAL prediction. Earlier modeling found negligible benefit from\nincreasing its direction-table capacity, so another direction-only\nchange is unlikely to clear the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold.\nFetch already computes the direct target and sequential successor,\nbut EX recomputes PC-relative targets and PC+4 while verifying the\naccepted prediction. Reusing registered successors can reduce target\narithmetic, mux depth and routing in the predictor correction path\nwithout adding a pipeline stage or a recovery cycle.\n\nNotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"The current predictor already handles direct branches and JALs. Prior experiments found almost no benefit from a larger direction table, and moving target arithmetic into fetch did not improve fitness. I’m checking the remaining JALR traffic to assess indirect-target prediction.\n"}}
