{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(json.dumps({k:d.get(k) for k in ('id','title','outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles')},indent=2))\n  print('MOTIVATION:',d.get('motivation',''))\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r6s2','r7s2')):\n   print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261001-003-r1s2\",\n  \"title\": \"64-entry folded-PC bimodal prediction at fetch\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 13.51,\n  \"delta_pct\": 11.47,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 5.66,\n  \"cycles\": 4190307\n}\nMOTIVATION: The baseline has no branch prediction: every aligned taken conditional\nbranch and every JAL/JALR redirects in EX and discards the current fetch.\nIF/ID is combinational, so a correctly predicted direct transfer can\neliminate the one-cycle recovery bubble without adding a pipeline stage.\nCoreMark repeatedly executes loops and direct calls, making a small\nadaptive direction predictor a promising way to increase iterations/cycle.\nProfiling the canonical 2K, -O3, 10-iteration instruction stream between\nits MMIO markers finds 529588 conditional branches, of which 294425 are\ntaken, plus 49367 direct JALs. A 64-entry table with a folded PC index\npredicts about 92.2 percent of conditional branches correctly.\nThe measured critical path is the forwarded combinational M-extension\nALU, not branch lookup; this proposal targets throughput while keeping\npredictor storage and added fetch logic small enough to preserve Fmax.\n\nNOTES: \n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261001-003-r2s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Compact bimodal direct-transfer prediction on the registered-divider champion\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 75.79,\n  \"delta_pct\": 11.18,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 31.759,\n  \"cycles\": 4190281\n}\nMOTIVATION: The current champion has the registered DIV/REM unit but still fetches\nsequentially and loses one fetch cycle on every taken branch and jump.\nLESSONS.md and the hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 experiment show that a 64-entry\nfolded-PC bimodal predictor plus direct JAL prediction reduced canonical\nCoreMark's bracketed cycles from 4491485 to 4190307, a 7.19 percent\nthroughput gain. That attempt used the old combinational divider and\ntherefore achieved only 5.66 MHz. The champion achieves 30.617 MHz with\nthe same 4491485-cycle window, so its absence of prediction leaves this\nmeasured throughput opportunity available. This proposal applies the\npredictor\n…[11789 of 14861 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a2fe82a932405d85]…\nnonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers and three-placement median\nGowin timing; inspect that the new boundaries remain in the netlist.\n\nNOTES: \n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261001-003-r8s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Direction-specific predictor recovery rails into one fetch PC\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 171.85,\n  \"delta_pct\": 6.87,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 75.24,\n  \"cycles\": 4378154\n}\nMOTIVATION: The champion scores 160.81 at 70.404 MHz and 4378154 CoreMark marker\ncycles. Its worst reported path starts at MEM/WB load data, crosses\nID/EX operand delivery and EX, and ends at the IF PC register. Earlier\nprofiling puts the maximum remaining prediction-accuracy cycle benefit\nnear 1.3 percent, below the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. Local BHT\nupdates, carried successors and split normal/recovery PC registers have\nnot produced an accepted improvement. The promising predictor angle is\nto shorten the existing same-cycle correction selection, without adding\na recovery stage, another public address mux or a larger predictor.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
