{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_34", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r9s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r9s2\ntitle: \"Bounded direct-target arithmetic in the fetch predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion already predicts direct JALs and uses a 64-entry folded-PC\n  bimodal table. Prior predictor profiling found only 41558 conditional\n  direction errors and 13735 JALRs in the timed CoreMark trace; eliminating\n  all of them would save approximately 1.3 percent of its 4378154 marker\n  cycles, below the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. LUT-RAM counters,\n  local counter feedback and split recovery PC registers did not improve\n  fitness. Fetch currently computes a full 32-bit PC-relative sum and then\n  reduces its upper twelve bits before selecting a speculative successor,\n  although speculation accepts only aligned targets below 1 MiB. Replace\n  that arithmetic and its range check with an exact bounded calculation\n  while retaining the current predictor decisions and recovery schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the existing BHT, direct-transfer predecode, single full-width PC\n  register and EX recovery predicates. Compute the speculative direct\n  target's low twenty bits with a 21-bit unsigned addition of pc[19:0]\n  and direct_imm[19:0], retaining its carry c. Both B and J immediates\n  have direct_imm[31:20] equal to twelve copies of instruction bit 31;\n  call that sign bit s. The original 32-bit sum has zero upper bits\n  exactly when one of these disjoint conditions holds:\n    pc[31:20] == 0     and c == s;\n    pc[31:20] == 1     and s == 1 and c == 0;\n    pc[31:20] == 0xfff and s == 0 and c == 1.\n  Predecode the three PC-high cases directly from the registered PC,\n  independently of the immediate addition. Use this predicate and the\n  low sum's alignment to qualify the original JAL/BHT direction decision.\n  On an accepted prediction the candidate is {12'b0, low_sum[19:0]}.\n  This removes the upper twelve carry stages and post-adder upper-bit\n  reduction from the instruction-to-predicted-PC cone. Preserve modulo\n  2^32 behavior, including wraparound and PCs outside instruction RAM\n  w\n…[1258 of 4330 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7b5553611c922a80]…\nsh that the predictor range check no longer contains a 32-bit\n  addition; EX recovery or divider recurrence may still limit the gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace speculative full-width direct-target addition and its upper\n      twelve-bit zero test with a low-twenty-bit sum plus exact sign/carry\n      and predecoded high-PC range qualification. Preserve all prediction\n      decisions, the full-width PC, sequential increment and correction\n      priority, BHT training and the internal IF interface.\n  - file: test/test_if_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing assertions and add directed and randomized reference\n      comparisons against full modulo-2^32 B/J target arithmetic. Exercise\n      sign/carry combinations at PC-high values 0, 1, 0xfff and other\n      values, out-of-range PCs that enter RAM, wraparound, alignment and\n      stalled or squashed lookups with simultaneous training and recovery.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
